window.MUSTASFA_DATA={"version":"0.6","title":"al-Mustaṣfā min ʿIlm al-Uṣūl","subtitle":"Critical Arabic–English working reader · original translation & cross-work concordance","status":"FULL ARABIC DIGITAL WITNESS • SELECTED TECHNICAL ENGLISH TRANSLATION • NOT A COMPLETED BOOK TRANSLATION","edited_at":"2026-10-19","sections":[{"id":"R01","part":"preface","group":"Sequential Arabic collation • new translation","title":"The transmitted sciences and the mixed character of jurisprudence","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P003","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"ونقلي محض كالأحاديث والتفاسير، والخطب في أمثالها يسير؛ إذ يستوي في الاستقلال بها الصغير والكبير؛ لأن قوة الحفظ كافية في النقل وليس فيها مجال للعقل. وأشرف العلوم ما ازدوج فيه العقل والسمع واصطحب فيه الرأي والشرع، وعلم الفقه وأصوله من هذا القبيل فإنه يأخذ من صفو الشرع والعقل سواء السبيل، فلا هو تصرف بمحض العقول بحيث لا يتلقاه الشرع بالقبول ولا هو مبني على محض التقليد الذي لا يشهد له العقل بالتأييد والتسديد.","en":"A second kind is purely transmitted, such as prophetic reports and Qurʾānic commentaries. The task involved in such disciplines is comparatively slight, since young and old can stand on equal footing in mastering them: strength of memory suffices for transmission, which, as such, affords no exercise to reason. The noblest sciences, however, are those in which intellect and transmitted authority are joined, and considered judgment accompanies revealed law. Jurisprudence and its principles belong to this class. They draw a straight course from the choicest elements of both revelation and reason. They are neither an exercise of unaided intellect which revelation cannot accept, nor a structure built upon mere imitation without the confirming and correcting witness of reason.","terms":["fiqh","usul","kalam"],"notes":[{"type":"textual","label":"Status of this translation","text":"A new English translation of a consecutive Arabic paragraph from the identified OpenITI digital witness. Hammad has not been systematically collated with this passage and any future critical text may require emendation."},{"type":"scholarship","label":"Reason and authoritative transmission","text":"Here al-ʿaql, al-samʿ, al-raʾy and al-sharʿ are four interrelated expressions and must not be flattened into a simple secular reason versus religious tradition opposition."}],"coverage":"complete source paragraph","arabicWitness":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","collationStatus":"Arabic digital witness matched; not independently proofed against print or Hammad"},{"id":"R02","part":"preface","group":"Sequential Arabic collation • new translation","title":"How al-Ghazālī situates his earlier writings","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P003","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"ولأجل شرف علم الفقه وسببه وفر الله دواعي الخلق على طلبه وكان العلماء به أرفع العلماء مكانا وأجلهم شأنا وأكثرهم أتباعا وأعوانا؛ فتقاضاني في عنفوان شبابي اختصاص هذا العلم بفوائد الدين والدنيا وثواب الآخرة والأولى أن أصرف إليه من مهلة العمر صدرا وأن أخص به من متنفس الحياة قدرا، فصنفت كتبا كثيرة في فروع الفقه وأصوله، ثم أقبلت بعده على علم طريق الآخرة ومعرفة أسرار الدين الباطنة فصنفت فيه كتبا بسيطة ككتاب \" إحياء علوم الدين \" ووجيزة ككتاب جواهر القرآن ووسيطة ككتاب كيمياء السعادة.","en":"Because of the excellence of jurisprudence and what gives it that excellence, God has made people eager to seek it; its scholars enjoy the highest rank and standing, and gather the greatest number of followers and supporters. In my early years, therefore, the distinctive benefits of this discipline for religion and worldly life, and the recompense of the next life and this, impelled me to devote the first portion of my allotted time to it and to reserve part of my remaining breath for its study. I composed many books on the detailed rulings of law and on its principles. Thereafter I turned to knowledge of the path to the afterlife and acquaintance with religion’s inward mysteries, and in that field composed extensive books, such as the Revival of the Religious Sciences, concise ones, such as The Jewels of the Qurʾān, and works of intermediate extent, such as The Alchemy of Happiness.","terms":["usul","ilm_batin"],"notes":[{"type":"textual","label":"Status of this translation","text":"A new English translation of a consecutive Arabic paragraph from the identified OpenITI digital witness. Hammad has not been systematically collated with this passage and any future critical text may require emendation."},{"type":"scholarship","label":"Explicit authorial bibliography","text":"The three named works are securely identified by al-Ghazālī himself. Their being named here does not, by itself, establish a technical equivalence between their vocabulary and that of al-Mustaṣfā."}],"coverage":"complete source paragraph","arabicWitness":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","collationStatus":"Arabic digital witness matched; not independently proofed against print or Hammad"},{"id":"R03","part":"preface","group":"Sequential Arabic collation • new translation","title":"A deliberate middle course between earlier uṣūl works","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P003","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"ثم ساقني قدر الله تعالى إلى معاودة التدريس والإفادة، فاقترح علي طائفة من محصلي علم الفقه تصنيفا في أصول الفقه أصرف العناية فيه إلى التلفيق بين الترتيب والتحقيق وإلى التوسط بين الإخلال والإملال على وجه يقع في الفهم دون كتاب \" تهذيب الأصول \" لميله إلى الاستقصاء والاستكثار، وفوق كتاب \" المنخول \" لميله إلى الإيجاز والاختصار، فأجبتهم إلى ذلك مستعينا بالله، وجمعت فيه بين الترتيب والتحقيق لفهم المعاني فلا مندوحة لأحدهما عن الثاني، فصنفته وأتيت فيه بترتيب لطيف عجيب يطلع الناظر في أول وهلة على جميع مقاصد هذا العلم ويفيده الاحتواء على جميع مسارح النظر فيه، فكل علم لا يستولي الطالب في ابتداء نظره على مجامعه ولا مبانيه فلا مطمع له في الظفر بأسراره ومباغيه؛ وقد سميته كتاب \" المستصفى من علم الأصول والله تعالى هو المسئول لينعم بالتوفيق ويهدي إلى سواء الطريق وهو بإجابة السائلين حقيق.","en":"God’s decree then brought me back to teaching and instruction. A group of advanced students of jurisprudence asked me to compose a treatise on legal theory in which I would unite systematic arrangement with rigorous examination, steering a middle course between a treatment too defective through omission and one too wearisome through excess. It was to be easier to grasp than The Refinement of Legal Principles, which inclines towards thoroughness and extensive treatment, but fuller than The Sifted Summary, which inclines towards brevity and condensation. I accepted their request with God’s assistance. I joined orderly arrangement to critical investigation, since the understanding of meanings cannot dispense with either. I therefore composed the work with an elegant and remarkable organization, so that at first sight the reader might grasp all the aims of the discipline and survey the entire field in which inquiry may proceed. Unless a student first gains command of a science’s principal divisions and foundations, there is no hope of reaching its deeper inquiries and ultimate goals. I have named it The Quintessence of the Science of Legal Principles [al-Mustaṣfā min ʿIlm al-Uṣūl]. It is God who is asked to grant success and guide us to the straight path; He is indeed the one who answers those who ask.","terms":["usul","fiqh"],"notes":[{"type":"textual","label":"Status of this translation","text":"A new English translation of a consecutive Arabic paragraph from the identified OpenITI digital witness. Hammad has not been systematically collated with this passage and any future critical text may require emendation."},{"type":"scholarship","label":"Named earlier treatises","text":"al-Tahdhīb and al-Mankhūl are explicit authorial references. The words tartīb and taḥqīq signify systematic ordering and stringent examination in this compositional context, not two named schools of jurisprudence."}],"coverage":"complete source paragraph","arabicWitness":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","collationStatus":"Arabic digital witness matched; not independently proofed against print or Hammad"},{"id":"R04","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential Arabic collation • new translation","title":"The opening: prolegomenon and four axes","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P004","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"صدر الكتاب اعلم أن هذا العلم الملقب بأصول الفقه قد رتبناه وجمعناه في هذا الكتاب وبنيناه على مقدمة وأربعة أقطاب، المقدمة لها كالتوطئة والتمهيد، والأقطاب هي المشتملة على لباب المقصود. ولنذكر في صدر الكتاب معنى أصول الفقه وحده وحقيقته أولا، ثم مرتبته ونسبته إلى العلوم ثانيا، ثم كيفية انشعابه به إلى هذه المقدمة والأقطاب الأربعة ثالثا، ثم كيفية اندراج جميع أقسامه وتفاصيله تحت الأقطاب الأربعة رابعا، ثم وجه تعلقه بهذه المقدمة خامسا. بيان حد أصول الفقه","en":"Opening of the book. Know that the discipline called the principles of jurisprudence has been ordered and collected in this book and built upon a preliminary introduction and four axes. The introduction serves as a preparation and preface, while the axes contain the substance of what is intended. At the outset we shall discuss, first, the meaning of uṣūl al-fiqh, its definition, and its reality; second, its standing and relation to the sciences; third, how it branches into this introduction and the four axes; fourth, how all its divisions and details fall under these four axes; and fifth, how it is related to the introduction. On defining the principles of jurisprudence.","terms":["usul","qutb","hadd"],"notes":[{"type":"textual","label":"Status of this translation","text":"A new English translation of a consecutive Arabic paragraph from the identified OpenITI digital witness. Hammad has not been systematically collated with this passage and any future critical text may require emendation."},{"type":"scholarship","label":"Quṭb and the form of the science","text":"The word quṭb here functions as the organizing axis or pivot of inquiry, not merely as a generic chapter or a metaphysical centre. The same classificatory structure is inherited and reworked in Ibn Rushd’s al-Ḍarūrī."}],"coverage":"complete source paragraph","arabicWitness":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","collationStatus":"Arabic digital witness matched; not independently proofed against print or Hammad"},{"id":"R05","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential Arabic collation • new translation","title":"Technical fiqh versus ordinary understanding","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P004","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"اعلم أنك لا تفهم معنى أصول الفقه ما لم تعرف أولا معنى الفقه، والفقه عبارة عن العلم والفهم في أصل الوضع، يقال فلان يفقه الخير والشر أي يعلمه ويفهمه، ولكن صار بعرف العلماء عبارة عن العلم بالأحكام الشرعية الثابتة لأفعال المكلفين خاصة، حتى لا يطلق بحكم العادة اسم الفقيه على متكلم وفلسفي ونحوي ومحدث ومفسر بل يختص بالعلماء بالأحكام الشرعية الثابتة للأفعال الإنسانية كالوجوب والحظر والإباحة والندب والكراهة وكون العقد صحيحا وفاسدا وباطلا وكون العبادة قضاء وأداء وأمثاله. ولا يخفى عليك أن للأفعال أحكاما عقلية أي مدركة بالعقل ككونها أعراضا وقائمة بالمحل ومخالفة للجوهر وكونها أكوانا حركة وسكونا وأمثالها، والعارف بذلك يسمى متكلما لا فقيها.","en":"Understand that you cannot grasp the meaning of the principles of jurisprudence without first knowing what jurisprudence (fiqh) means. In its original linguistic sense, fiqh means knowledge and understanding: one says that a person understands good and evil, meaning that he knows and comprehends them. But in scholars’ established usage it has come to mean, specifically, knowledge of the revealed legal qualifications pertaining to the acts of those subject to the Law. Thus ordinary usage does not apply the name jurist (faqīh) to a theologian, philosopher, grammarian, scholar of transmitted reports, or exegete. It is reserved for those who know the legal qualifications of human acts—obligation, prohibition, permission, recommendation and reprehensibility; whether a contract is sound, defective, or void; whether an act of worship is performance within its time or discharge afterwards, and the like. You will also appreciate that acts have rationally ascertainable predicates, such as being accidents, inhering in a substrate, differing from substance, or constituting motion and rest. Someone who knows these is called a speculative theologian, not a jurist.","terms":["fiqh","hukm","taklif","wajib","mandub","sihha","fasad"],"notes":[{"type":"textual","label":"Status of this translation","text":"A new English translation of a consecutive Arabic paragraph from the identified OpenITI digital witness. Hammad has not been systematically collated with this passage and any future critical text may require emendation."},{"type":"scholarship","label":"Aḥkām are relational qualifications","text":"Translate aḥkām here as legal qualifications or legal rulings depending on grammatical context; do not take all these as properties inherent in the physical act. Technical distinctions between fāsid and bāṭil require school- and context-specific notes."}],"coverage":"complete source paragraph","arabicWitness":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","collationStatus":"Arabic digital witness matched; not independently proofed against print or Hammad"},{"id":"R06","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential Arabic collation • new translation","title":"Uṣūl is about proof and indication at the general level","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P004","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"وأما أحكامها من حيث إنها واجبة ومحظورة ومباحة ومكروهة ومندوب إليها فإنما يتولى الفقيه بيانها، فإذا فهمت هذا فافهم أن أصول الفقه عبارة عن أدلة هذه الأحكام وعن معرفة وجوه دلالتها على الأحكام من حيث الجملة لا من حيث التفصيل، فإن علم الخلاف من الفقه أيضا مشتمل على أدلة الأحكام ووجوه دلالتها ولكن من حيث التفصيل، كدلالة حديث خاص في مسألة النكاح بلا ولي على الخصوص ودلالة آية خاصة في مسألة متروك التسمية على الخصوص.","en":"It is the jurist who explains the legal qualifications of acts as obligatory, forbidden, permitted, reprehensible, or recommended. Once you have understood that, understand also that uṣūl al-fiqh is concerned with the proofs of these rulings and with knowing the ways in which those proofs indicate the rulings—considered in general, not in the detailed treatment of particular cases. The discipline of juristic disagreement (ʿilm al-khilāf), which itself belongs to jurisprudence, likewise deals with the proofs of rulings and their modes of indication, but in relation to individual questions: for example, what a particular report indicates in the dispute over marriage without a guardian, or what a particular verse indicates for the case of slaughter without pronouncing God’s name.","terms":["fiqh","usul","dalala","hukm"],"notes":[{"type":"textual","label":"Status of this translation","text":"A new English translation of a consecutive Arabic paragraph from the identified OpenITI digital witness. Hammad has not been systematically collated with this passage and any future critical text may require emendation."},{"type":"scholarship","label":"Delimiting uṣūl from khilāf","text":"This contrasts general modalities of legal evidence with evidential debate over particular rulings. It is not a contrast between theory that never uses concrete examples and practice that always does."}],"coverage":"complete source paragraph","arabicWitness":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","collationStatus":"Arabic digital witness matched; not independently proofed against print or Hammad"},{"id":"R07","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential Arabic collation • new translation","title":"The three evidentiary foundations and methods of indication","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P004–V01P005","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"وأما الأصول فلا يتعرض فيها لإحدى المسائل ولا على طريق ضرب المثال، بل يتعرض فيها لأصل الكتاب والسنة والإجماع ولشرائط صحتها وثبوتها ثم لوجوه دلالتها الجملية إما من حيث صيغتها أو مفهوم لفظها أو مجرى لفظها أو معقول لفظها وهو القياس من غير أن يتعرض فيها لمسألة خاصة فبهذا تفارق أصول الفقه فروعه وقد عرفت من هذا أن أدلة الأحكام الكتاب والسنة والإجماع، فالعلم بطرق ثبوت هذه الأصول الثلاثة وشروط صحتها ووجوه دلالتها على الأحكام هو العلم الذي يعبر عنه بأصول الفقه","en":"The principles, by contrast, do not take up an individual legal question as their subject, even in the manner of a worked example. Rather, their proper inquiry is into the foundation of the Book, the Sunna and consensus, the conditions of their authenticity and establishment, and then their general ways of indicating rulings: through linguistic form, through what the expression conveys by implication, through the course and requirements of the expression, or through the intelligible meaning discerned from it—that is, qiyās. They do not undertake the resolution of a particular legal question. This is what distinguishes the principles of jurisprudence from its branches. You can therefore see that the evidences of rulings are the Book, the Sunna and consensus; the discipline concerned with how these three foundations are established, with the conditions of their soundness, and with the ways in which they indicate rulings is what is meant by uṣūl al-fiqh.","terms":["usul","ijma","dalala","qiyas","hukm"],"notes":[{"type":"textual","label":"Status of this translation","text":"A new English translation of a consecutive Arabic paragraph from the identified OpenITI digital witness. Hammad has not been systematically collated with this passage and any future critical text may require emendation."},{"type":"scholarship","label":"Qiyās here is an inference from intelligible meaning","text":"Rather than impose “analogy” on every use of qiyās, translate it here according to its position among modes of textual indication. Opwis 2019, pp. 93–112, distinguishes inferential form from the epistemic standing of its premises."}],"coverage":"complete source paragraph","arabicWitness":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","collationStatus":"Arabic digital witness matched; not independently proofed against print or Hammad"},{"id":"R08","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential Arabic collation • new translation","title":"The sciences and the discipline of inward purification","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P005","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"اعلم أن العلوم تنقسم إلى عقلية كالطب والحساب والهندسة وليس ذلك من غرضنا. وإلى دينية كالكلام والفقه وأصوله وعلم الحديث وعلم التفسير وعلم الباطن، أعني علم القلب وتطهيره عن الأخلاق الذميمة وكل واحد من العقلية والدينية ينقسم إلى كلية وجزئية.","en":"Understand that the sciences divide into rational disciplines, such as medicine, arithmetic and geometry—which are not our present concern—and religious disciplines, such as speculative theology (kalām), jurisprudence and its principles, the study of prophetic reports, Qurʾānic exegesis, and knowledge of the inward life: by the latter I mean knowledge of the heart and its purification from blameworthy character traits. Both the rational and the religious sciences can, in turn, be divided into comprehensive and particular disciplines.","terms":["kalam","fiqh","usul","ilm_batin","kulli"],"notes":[{"type":"textual","label":"Status of this translation","text":"A new English translation of a consecutive Arabic paragraph from the identified OpenITI digital witness. Hammad has not been systematically collated with this passage and any future critical text may require emendation."},{"type":"scholarship","label":"What ʿilm al-bāṭin denotes here","text":"The text expressly glosses “inward knowledge” as the science of the heart and its purification from blameworthy dispositions. Do not import a general esoteric doctrine merely from the expression."}],"coverage":"complete source paragraph","arabicWitness":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","collationStatus":"Arabic digital witness matched; not independently proofed against print or Hammad"},{"id":"R09","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential Arabic collation • new translation","title":"Why kalām is classed as comprehensive","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P005","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"فالعلم الكلي من العلوم الدينية هو الكلام وسائر العلوم من الفقه وأصوله والحديث والتفسير علوم جزئية، لأن المفسر لا ينظر إلا في معنى الكتاب خاصة والمحدث لا ينظر إلا في طريق ثبوت الحديث خاصة، والفقيه لا ينظر إلا في أحكام أفعال المكلفين خاصة، والأصولي لا ينظر إلا في أدلة الأحكام الشرعية خاصة والمتكلم هو الذي ينظر في أعم الأشياء وهو الموجود، فيقسم الموجود أولا إلى قديم حادث، ثم يقسم المحدث إلى جوهر وعرض، ثم يقسم العرض إلى ما تشترط فيه الحياة من العلم والإرادة والقدرة والكلام والسمع والبصر وإلى ما يستغني عنها كاللون والريح والطعم، ويقسم الجوهر إلى الحيوان والنبات والجماد ويبين أن اختلافها بالأنواع أو بالأعراض.","en":"Within the religious sciences, kalām is the comprehensive discipline; the others—jurisprudence, its principles, the science of reports and exegesis—are particular sciences. For the exegete considers specifically the meaning of the Book; the traditionist the way in which a report is established; the jurist the legal qualifications of the acts of those subject to the Law; and the uṣūlī the proofs of revealed rulings. The speculative theologian, by contrast, considers what is most universal: being (al-mawjūd). He first divides what exists into eternal and originated; then originated being into substance and accident; then accidents into those requiring life—such as knowledge, will, power, speech, hearing and sight—and those not requiring it, such as colour, smell and taste. He divides substances into animals, plants and inanimate bodies, and examines whether their distinctions are differences of species or of accidents.","terms":["kalam","kulli","usul","fiqh","hukm"],"notes":[{"type":"textual","label":"Status of this translation","text":"A new English translation of a consecutive Arabic paragraph from the identified OpenITI digital witness. Hammad has not been systematically collated with this passage and any future critical text may require emendation."},{"type":"scholarship","label":"Universality and ontological vocabulary","text":"Al-mawjūd, jawhar and ʿaraḍ are technical terms in Ashʿarī kalām, not direct equivalents of Heideggerian Being or the Aristotelian substance-accident contrast without historical qualification."}],"coverage":"complete source paragraph","arabicWitness":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","collationStatus":"Arabic digital witness matched; not independently proofed against print or Hammad"},{"id":"R10","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential Arabic collation • new translation","title":"The relative autonomy of legal theory","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P006","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"فإذا الكلام هو المتكفل بإثبات مبادئ العلوم الدينية كلها، فهي جزئية بالإضافة إلى الكلام، فالكلام هو العلم الأعلى في الرتبة إذ منه النزول إلى هذه الجزئيات فإن قيل: فليكن من شرط الأصولي والفقيه والمفسر والمحدث أن يكون قد حصل علم الكلام لأنه قبل الفراغ من الكلي الأعلى كيف يمكنه النزول إلى الجزئي الأسفل؟ قلنا: ليس ذلك شرطا في كونه أصوليا وفقيها ومفسرا ومحدثا وإن كان ذلك شرطا في كونه عالما مطلقا مليئا بالعلوم الدينية، وذلك أنه ما من علم من العلوم الجزئية إلا وله مباد تؤخذ مسلمة بالتقليد في ذلك العلم ويطلب برهان ثبوتها في علم آخر.","en":"Kalām, then, undertakes to establish the first principles of all the religious sciences, which are particular in relation to it. In this respect kalām ranks as the highest of the sciences, since one descends from it to these particular disciplines. An objection may be raised: Should it not then be a condition of being an uṣūlī, jurist, exegete or traditionist that one have mastered kalām? How could someone descend to a lower, particular science without first completing the higher, comprehensive one? We reply: this is not a condition for being an uṣūlī, jurist, exegete or traditionist, though it is a condition for being a scholar in the unrestricted sense, fully equipped with the religious sciences. Every particular science has first principles accepted as given within it, whose demonstration is sought in some other science.","terms":["kalam","usul","kulli","fiqh","burhan"],"notes":[{"type":"textual","label":"Status of this translation","text":"A new English translation of a consecutive Arabic paragraph from the identified OpenITI digital witness. Hammad has not been systematically collated with this passage and any future critical text may require emendation."},{"type":"scholarship","label":"Principles borrowed from another discipline","text":"This is a technical argument about the explanatory order of the sciences. The acceptance of a premise inside one science does not establish that it cannot be demonstratively justified elsewhere."}],"coverage":"complete source paragraph","arabicWitness":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","collationStatus":"Arabic digital witness matched; not independently proofed against print or Hammad"},{"id":"R11","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential Arabic collation • new translation","title":"The jurist’s and uṣūlī’s borrowed premises","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P006","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"فالفقيه ينظر في نسبة فعل المكلف إلى خطاب الشرع في أمره ونهيه وليس عليه إقامة البرهان على إثبات الأفعال الاختياريات للمكلفين فقد أنكرت الجبرية فعل الإنسان وأنكرت طائفة وجود الأعراض والفعل عرض. ولا على الفقيه إقامة البرهان على ثبوت خطاب الشرع وأن لله تعالى كلاما قائما بنفسه هو أمر ونهي، ولكن يأخذ ثبوت الخطاب من الله تعالى وثبوت الفعل من المكلف على سبيل التقليد وينظر في نسبة الفعل إلى الخطاب فيكون قد قام بمنتهى علمه. وكذلك الأصولي يأخذ بالتقليد من المتكلم أن قول الرسول حجة ودليل واجب الصدق، ثم ينظر في وجوه دلالته وشروط صحته.","en":"The jurist investigates the relation of a legally accountable person’s act to the Law’s address, commanding or forbidding it. He is not required to demonstrate that human beings perform voluntary acts—although the determinists have denied human agency, while another group has denied accidents, and an act is an accident. Nor is the jurist obliged to prove the existence of a revealed legal address or the doctrine that God possesses speech subsisting in Him which commands and forbids. Instead, he takes as granted the existence of God’s address and the act of the accountable person, and studies the relation between them. In doing so he has reached the proper terminus of his discipline. Likewise, the uṣūlī accepts from the theologian, without himself demonstrating it there, that the Messenger’s utterance is authoritative proof whose truth must be acknowledged; he then investigates its modes of indication and the conditions of its authenticity.","terms":["fiqh","usul","kalam","taklif","khitab","hujjiyya","dalala"],"notes":[{"type":"textual","label":"Status of this translation","text":"A new English translation of a consecutive Arabic paragraph from the identified OpenITI digital witness. Hammad has not been systematically collated with this passage and any future critical text may require emendation."},{"type":"scholarship","label":"Taqlīd inside an ordered science","text":"Taqlīd here means taking a science’s borrowed foundations as granted for the purposes of its specific inquiry, not a blanket rejection of reason. The paragraph’s theological doctrines are al-Ghazālī’s claims, not editorial endorsements."}],"coverage":"complete source paragraph","arabicWitness":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","collationStatus":"Arabic digital witness matched; not independently proofed against print or Hammad"},{"id":"R12","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential Arabic collation • new translation","title":"The four parts follow from derivation itself","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P006","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"بيان كيفية دورانه على الأقطاب الأربعة اعلم أنك إذا فهمت أن نظر الأصولي في وجوه دلالة الأدلة السمعية على الأحكام الشرعية لم يخف عليك أن المقصود معرفة كيفية اقتباس الأحكام من الأدلة، فوجب النظر في الأحكام، ثم في الأدلة وأقسامها، ثم في كيفية اقتباس الأحكام من الأدلة، ثم في صفات المقتبس الذي له أن يقتبس الأحكام، فإن الأحكام ثمرات وكل ثمرة فلها صفة وحقيقة في نفسها ولها مثمر ومستثمر وطريق في الاستثمار.","en":"On how the science turns upon its four axes. Once you understand that the uṣūlī investigates how transmitted proofs indicate legal rulings, you will see that the object is to know how to draw rulings from their proofs. We must therefore consider, first, the rulings themselves; second, the proofs and their classes; third, how rulings are drawn from proofs; and fourth, the qualities of the one entitled to draw them. For rulings are fruits, and every fruit has a character and nature of its own, a source that produces it, someone who harvests it, and a manner of harvesting.","terms":["usul","dalala","hukm","qutb","mujtahid"],"notes":[{"type":"textual","label":"Status of this translation","text":"A new English translation of a consecutive Arabic paragraph from the identified OpenITI digital witness. Hammad has not been systematically collated with this passage and any future critical text may require emendation."},{"type":"scholarship","label":"Four axes versus four independent sources","text":"The image distinguishes object, authoritative evidence, inferential technique, and qualified knower; it does not mean that there are four coequal original sources of law."}],"coverage":"complete source paragraph","arabicWitness":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","collationStatus":"Arabic digital witness matched; not independently proofed against print or Hammad"},{"id":"R13","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential Arabic collation • new translation","title":"The fruits, the productive proofs, and the modes of drawing them","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P006","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"والثمرة هي الأحكام، أعني الوجوب والحظر والندب والكراهة والإباحة والحسن والقبح والقضاء والأداء والصحة والفساد وغيرها والمثمر هي الأدلة، وهي ثلاثة الكتاب والسنة والإجماع فقط. وطرق الاستثمار هي وجوه دلالة الأدلة وهي أربعة، إذ الأقوال إما أن تدل على الشيء بصيغتها ومنظومها، أو بفحواها ومفهومها وباقتضائها وضرورتها أو بمعقولها ومعناها المستنبط منها.","en":"The fruits are the rulings: obligation, prohibition, recommendation, reprehensibility, permission, the good and the bad, compensatory performance and timely performance, legal soundness and defectiveness, and other such qualifications. The productive sources are the proofs, and these are three only: the Book, the Sunna and consensus. The ways of harvesting are the modes by which proofs indicate their conclusions, and these are four: utterances indicate something either through their linguistic form and expressed wording; through what their sense and implication convey; through what they necessarily require or presuppose; or through the intelligible meaning inferred from them.","terms":["hukm","ijma","dalala","qiyas","sihha","fasad"],"notes":[{"type":"textual","label":"Status of this translation","text":"A new English translation of a consecutive Arabic paragraph from the identified OpenITI digital witness. Hammad has not been systematically collated with this passage and any future critical text may require emendation."},{"type":"scholarship","label":"Explicit expression, implication, necessity, intelligible meaning","text":"The source organizes four modes of signification. Do not assimilate these without qualification to a single modern linguistic taxonomy or to a claim that every example yields deductive certainty."}],"coverage":"complete source paragraph","arabicWitness":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","collationStatus":"Arabic digital witness matched; not independently proofed against print or Hammad"},{"id":"R14","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential Arabic collation • new translation","title":"The jurist as harvester and the four axes","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P006–V01P007","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"والمستثمر هو المجتهد، ولا بد من معرفة صفاته وشروطه وأحكامه. فإذا جملة الأصول تدور على أربعة أقطاب: القطب الأول: في الأحكام، والبداءة بها أولى لأنها الثمرة المطلوبة. القطب الثاني: في الأدلة، وهي الكتاب والسنة والإجماع وبها التثنية إذ بعد الفراغ من معرفة الثمرة لا أهم من معرفة المثمر. القطب الثالث: في طريق الاستثمار، وهو وجوه دلالة الأدلة، وهي أربعة: دلالة بالمنظوم، ودلالة بالمفهوم، ودلالة بالضرورة والاقتضاء، ودلالة بالمعنى المعقول. القطب الرابع: في المستثمر وهو المجتهد الذي يحكم بظنه، ويقابله المقلد الذي يلزمه اتباعه، فيجب ذكر شروط المقلد والمجتهد وصفاتهما.","en":"The harvester is the independent jurist (mujtahid), whose qualities, conditions and legal position must be known. Accordingly, the entire discipline of legal theory turns upon four axes. The first concerns rulings; we begin with them because they are the fruit sought. The second concerns the proofs—the Book, the Sunna and consensus. They come second because, once the fruit is known, nothing is more important than knowing the source that produces it. The third concerns the manner of harvesting, that is, the modes of indication of proofs: indication through explicit wording, through implication, through necessary presupposition and entailment, and through an intelligible inferred meaning. The fourth concerns the harvester, the mujtahid, who renders judgment according to his preponderant estimation (ẓann). Opposite him stands the follower (muqallid), who is bound to follow the jurist. We must therefore discuss the conditions and qualifications of both.","terms":["mujtahid","muqallid","ijtihad","zann","qutb","dalala"],"notes":[{"type":"textual","label":"Status of this translation","text":"A new English translation of a consecutive Arabic paragraph from the identified OpenITI digital witness. Hammad has not been systematically collated with this passage and any future critical text may require emendation."},{"type":"scholarship","label":"Ẓann is a technical inferential status","text":"The expression yaḥkumu bi-ẓannihi should be rendered as a ruling reached on the basis of predominant yet fallible judgment, not as an arbitrary guess. Compare Opwis (2019), 103–108, and Hallaq’s discussion of legal epistemology."}],"coverage":"complete source paragraph","arabicWitness":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","collationStatus":"Arabic digital witness matched; not independently proofed against print or Hammad"},{"id":"R15","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential opening collation · Gnamma v0.6","title":"The four axes comprehend the scattered chapters of uṣūl","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P007","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"لعلك تقول أصول الفقه تشتمل على أبواب كثيرة وفصول منتشرة فكيف يندرج جملتها تحت هذه الأقطاب الأربعة؟ فنقول: القطب الأول: هو الحكم.","en":"You might ask: “The principles of jurisprudence contain many chapters and dispersed subdivisions. How can all of them fall under these four axes?” We reply: the first axis concerns the legal ruling (al-ḥukm).","coverage":"complete source paragraph","terms":["usul","qutb","hukm"],"notes":[{"type":"Translation","label":"The organizing question","text":"Al-Ghazālī is offering a disciplinary architecture, not presenting four independent material sources of the law."}]},{"id":"R16","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential opening collation · Gnamma v0.6","title":"Ruler, addressee, subject act, and manifestation of the ruling","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P007","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"وللحكم حقيقة في نفسه وانقسام، وله تعلق بالحاكم وهو الشارع والمحكوم عليه وهو المكلف وبالمحكوم فيه وهو فعل المكلف وبالمظهر له وهو السبب والعلة","en":"A ruling has a determinate nature and divisions of its own. It is related to the one who lays it down—the Lawgiver (al-shāriʿ); the one upon whom it bears—the legally accountable person (al-mukallaf); that with which it is concerned—the accountable person’s act; and that by which it is made manifest—the occasioning condition (sabab) and the operative ground (ʿilla).","coverage":"selected excerpt","terms":["hukm","taklif","sabab","khitab"],"notes":[{"type":"Technical","label":"Relations, not properties","text":"The sequence is an analytic ordering of the ruling’s relations. Here sabab and ʿilla are connected with the manifestation of a rule; translating both as physical “cause” would mislead."},{"type":"Textual","label":"Extent of this extract","text":"This is an excerpt from a longer sentence; the remaining subdivisions are not silently paraphrased as though translated here."}]},{"id":"R17","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential opening collation · Gnamma v0.6","title":"The ruling is an address of revelation, not an attribute of the act","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P007","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"ففي البحث عن حقيقة الحكم في نفسه يتبين أنه عبارة عن خطاب الشرع وليس وصفا للفعل ولا حسن ولا قبح ولا مدخل للعقل فيه ولا حكم قبل ورود الشرع","en":"Investigation of the ruling’s nature shows that it consists in the address of revealed law (khiṭāb al-sharʿ), not in a quality belonging to the act itself—neither its goodness nor its badness. Reason does not constitute this ruling, and no such revealed-law ruling obtains before revelation has come.","coverage":"selected excerpt","terms":["hukm","khitab","kalam"],"notes":[{"type":"Doctrinal","label":"The Ashʿarī thesis in context","text":"The denial concerns constituting a binding revealed-law classification; it should not be generalized into a denial that reason recognizes truths or consequences of actions. Compare the chapter on ḥusn and qubḥ before revelation."}]},{"id":"R18","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential opening collation · Gnamma v0.6","title":"Required implication: what an utterance must presuppose","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P008","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"وأما الدلالة من حيث ضرورة اللفظ واقتضاؤه فيتضمن جملة من إشارات الألفاظ كقول القائل: أعتق عبدك عني، فتقول: أعتقت، فإنه يتضمن حصول الملك للملتمس ولم يتلفظا به لكنه من ضرورة ملفوظهما ومقتضاه.","en":"Indication through what an utterance necessarily presupposes and requires includes certain implications of what has been said. Suppose someone says: “Emancipate your slave on my behalf,” and you reply: “I have emancipated him.” These words entail the requester’s acquiring ownership, although neither speaker expressly mentioned it; that transfer is demanded by what they have uttered.","coverage":"selected excerpt","terms":["dalala","usul"],"notes":[{"type":"Technical","label":"Dalālat al-iqtiḍāʾ","text":"The inferred transaction is required for the utterance to be legally intelligible, rather than expressly stated in its words. “Implied meaning” without this necessity would be too broad."}]},{"id":"R19","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential opening collation · Gnamma v0.6","title":"Intelligible meaning and extension beyond the stated case","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P008","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"وأما الدلالة من حيث معقول اللفظ فهو كقوله - صلى الله عليه وسلم -: «لا يقضي القاضي وهو غضبان» فإنه يدل على الجائع والمريض والحاقن بمعقول معناه، ومنه ينشأ القياس وينجر إلى بيان جميع أحكام القياس وأقسامه.","en":"Indication through the intelligible import of an utterance is exemplified by the Prophet’s saying: “A judge must not adjudicate while angry.” Through its intelligible ground, the instruction extends also to someone distracted by hunger, illness, or an urgent need to relieve himself. From this sort of indication arises juridical qiyās, and from it follows the examination of the rulings and kinds of qiyās.","coverage":"complete source paragraph","terms":["dalala","qiyas"],"notes":[{"type":"Technical","label":"Ground of extension","text":"The extension is not a literal occurrence of the words “hunger” or “illness.” It rests on discerning the relevant ground and transferring the ruling. Compare the discussion of legal analogy and syllogistic form in Felicitas Opwis, “Syllogistic Logic in Islamic Legal Theory.”"}]},{"id":"R20","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential opening collation · Gnamma v0.6","title":"The triad of knowledge, proof, and ruling","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P008","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"اعلم أنه لما رجع حد أصول الفقه إلى معرفة أدلة الأحكام اشتمل الحد على ثلاثة ألفاظ: المعرفة والدليل والحكم فقالوا: إذا لم يكن بد من معرفة الحكم حتى كان معرفته أحد الأقطاب الأربعة فلا بد أيضا من معرفة الدليل ومعرفة المعرفة، أعني العلم. ثم العلم المطلوب لا وصول إليه إلا بالنظر، فلا بد من معرفة النظر","en":"Understand that, once the definition of uṣūl al-fiqh had been reduced to knowing the proofs of legal rulings, it contained three terms: knowledge, proof, and ruling. It was therefore argued that, just as a ruling must be understood—indeed, knowledge of rulings is one of the four axes—one must also understand proof and what it is to know, namely, knowledge (ʿilm). The knowledge sought here, moreover, is reached only by disciplined inquiry (naẓar), so inquiry too must be understood.","coverage":"selected excerpt","terms":["usul","hukm"],"notes":[{"type":"Technical","label":"Naẓar","text":"Naẓar is disciplined intellectual investigation; rendering it simply as “vision” or as an unqualified “rationalism” would suppress its methodological sense."}]},{"id":"R21","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential opening collation · Gnamma v0.6","title":"Which questions properly belong to uṣūl?","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P008","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"وأما معرفة حجية الإجماع وحجية القياس فذلك من خاصية أصول الفقه","en":"Establishing the probative authority (ḥujjiyya) of consensus (ijmāʿ) and of qiyās, by contrast, belongs specifically to the discipline of uṣūl al-fiqh.","coverage":"selected excerpt","terms":["ijma","qiyas","hujjiyya","usul"],"notes":[{"type":"Technical","label":"Ḥujjiyya","text":"A proof’s status as authoritative is a distinct question from the formal validity of an argument or the certainty of a premise."}]},{"id":"R22","part":"prelim","group":"Sequential opening collation · Gnamma v0.6","title":"Why a logical introduction is nevertheless retained","headingAr":"","sourcePage":"OpenITI V01P008","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","ar":"لكنا نقتصر من ذلك على ما تظهر فائدته على العموم في جملة العلوم من تعريف مدارك العقول وكيفية تدرجها من الضروريات إلى النظريات على وجه يتبين فيه حقيقة العلم والنظر والدليل وأقسامها وحججها تبيينا بليغا تخلو عنه مصنفات الكلام.","en":"We shall, however, confine ourselves to what has evident general benefit across the sciences: setting out the intellect’s modes of apprehension and the manner in which it advances from immediate or necessary cognitions (ḍarūriyyāt) to matters established through investigation (naẓariyyāt). This will clarify the nature of knowledge, inquiry, and proof, together with their divisions and warrants, more fully than is ordinarily found in works of kalām.","coverage":"selected excerpt","terms":["kalam","usul","burhan"],"notes":[{"type":"Method","label":"Limits of this quotation","text":"This passage explains why the logical introduction is useful generally. It is not a claim that all the material in that introduction belongs intrinsically to legal theory."}]},{"id":"P04","part":"preface","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Three kinds of knowledge","headingAr":"خطبة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:4","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/2/%D8%AE%D8%B7%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","ar":"ثم العلوم ثلاثة: عقلي محض لا يحث الشرع عليه ولا يندب إليه كالحساب والهندسة والنجوم وأمثاله من العلوم","en":"There are, then, three kinds of sciences. One is purely rational, and is not, as such, urged or recommended by the revealed Law—for example, arithmetic, geometry, and astronomy.","terms":["kalam"],"notes":[{"type":"context","label":"Classification, not a blanket rejection","text":"Read the surrounding passage: he distinguishes uncertain speculation from true knowledge lacking the benefit relevant to his argument; this is not an assessment of every mathematical practice."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"P05","part":"preface","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Knowledge transmitted without inference","headingAr":"خطبة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:4","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/2/%D8%AE%D8%B7%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","ar":"ونقلي محض كالأحاديث والتفاسير، والخطب في أمثالها يسير؛ إذ يستوي في الاستقلال بها الصغير والكبير؛ لأن قوة الحفظ كافية في النقل","en":"Another kind is purely transmitted, such as reports and commentarial traditions. Its acquisition, considered solely as transmission, demands little by way of argument: young and old alike can master it, since retention suffices for conveying it.","terms":["kalam"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"This is a formal contrast","text":"The claim concerns mere transmission, not every interpretative discipline of hadith or tafsīr."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"P06","part":"preface","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"The value of legal theory as a mixed science","headingAr":"خطبة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:4","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/2/%D8%AE%D8%B7%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","ar":"وأشرف العلوم ما ازدوج فيه العقل والسمع واصطحب فيه الرأي والشرع","en":"The noblest sciences are those in which reason and transmitted revelation are joined, and considered judgment accompanies the revealed Law.","terms":["usul","kalam"],"notes":[{"type":"crosswork","label":"A programmatic statement","text":"Compare his explicit acknowledgement of the inward sciences later in the preface rather than artificially opposing law to ethical or spiritual knowledge."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"P01","part":"preface","group":"Author’s preface","title":"The meeting of reason and revelation","headingAr":"خطبة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:4","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/2/%D8%AE%D8%B7%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","ar":"وأشرف العلوم ما ازدوج فيه العقل والسمع واصطحب فيه الرأي والشرع، وعلم الفقه وأصوله من هذا القبيل فإنه يأخذ من صفو الشرع والعقل سواء السبيل، فلا هو تصرف بمحض العقول بحيث لا يتلقاه الشرع بالقبول ولا هو مبني على محض التقليد الذي لا يشهد له العقل بالتأييد والتسديد.","en":"The noblest sciences are those in which reason and received revelation are joined, and reflective judgment accompanies the revealed Law. Jurisprudence and its principles belong to this kind: drawing from the clearest part of both revelation and reason, they follow an even course. They are neither an operation of unaided intellect that revelation would refuse to recognize, nor something resting on mere imitation without the confirmation and guidance of reason.","terms":["usul","kalam"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"ʿAql / samʿ / raʾy / sharʿ","text":"Al-Ghazālī juxtaposes four distinct terms. “Received revelation” renders samʿ here, in contrast with rational inquiry; it must not be silently reduced to isolated textual citations."},{"type":"context","label":"Spiritual and juridical reason","text":"The juxtaposition is authored in the preface and bears directly on comparisons with the logical and ethical works."},{"type":"translation-comparison","label":"With Hammad (1987), printed p. 303–304","text":"Hammad renders samʿ as “Tradition” and raʾy as rational opinion. This new rendering uses “received revelation” for samʿ and “reflective judgment” for raʾy to preserve both contrasts with ʿaql and sharʿ. The proposal is open to revision when the Arabic edition is fixed; it is not a correction of Hammad’s interpretation by fiat."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"P02","part":"preface","group":"Author’s preface","title":"Earlier theological, juristic, and spiritual writing","headingAr":"خطبة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:5","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/2/%D8%AE%D8%B7%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","ar":"فصنفت كتبا كثيرة في فروع الفقه وأصوله، ثم أقبلت بعده على علم طريق الآخرة ومعرفة أسرار الدين الباطنة فصنفت فيه كتبا بسيطة ككتاب إحياء علوم الدين ووجيزة ككتاب جواهر القرآن ووسيطة ككتاب كيمياء السعادة.","en":"I composed numerous books on the branches of jurisprudence and on its principles. I then turned to knowledge of the path to the Hereafter and the inward secrets of religion, producing expansive books in that field, such as the Revival of the Religious Sciences; concise books, such as The Jewels of the Qurʾān; and intermediate works, such as The Alchemy of Happiness.","terms":["usul"],"notes":[{"type":"crosswork","label":"Three explicitly named works","text":"The Iḥyāʾ, Jawāhir al-Qurʾān, and Kīmiyā-yi Saʿādat are named by al-Ghazālī in his own preface; these links are direct bibliographic evidence, not terminology-equivalence claims."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"P03","part":"preface","group":"Author’s preface","title":"Between excessive length and excessive brevity","headingAr":"خطبة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:5","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/2/%D8%AE%D8%B7%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","ar":"فاقترح علي طائفة من محصلي علم الفقه تصنيفا في أصول الفقه أصرف العناية فيه إلى التلفيق بين الترتيب والتحقيق وإلى التوسط بين الإخلال والإملال على وجه يقع في الفهم دون كتاب تهذيب الأصول لميله إلى الاستقصاء والاستكثار، وفوق كتاب المنخول لميله إلى الإيجاز والاختصار.","en":"A group of students accomplished in jurisprudence asked me for a work on legal principles that would unite careful arrangement with rigorous examination, and hold a middle course between harmful omission and wearisome elaboration. They wanted something accessible to understanding: less expansive than the Refinement of the Principles, which inclines to exhaustive treatment, but fuller than the Sifted Work, which inclines toward compression and brevity.","terms":["usul"],"notes":[{"type":"crosswork","label":"The author establishes his own sequence","text":"Al-Ghazālī explicitly compares the scope of al-Mustaṣfā with his Tahdhīb al-Uṣūl and al-Mankhūl, inviting diachronic collation of terminology and method."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"O01","part":"prelim","group":"Opening definition","title":"The design of the book","headingAr":"صدر الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:6","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/3/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%82%D9%87","ar":"اعلم أن هذا العلم الملقب بأصول الفقه قد رتبناه وجمعناه في هذا الكتاب وبنيناه على مقدمة وأربعة أقطاب، المقدمة لها كالتوطئة والتمهيد، والأقطاب هي المشتملة على لباب المقصود.","en":"Know that we have organized and assembled in this book the discipline called the principles of jurisprudence. We have built it upon an introduction and four principal axes. The introduction serves as preparation and groundwork, while the axes contain the substance of what we seek to establish.","terms":["usul","qutb"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"A conceptual architecture","text":"The axes (aqṭāb) organize relations among rule, evidence, inference, and the jurist, rather than simply listing types of evidence."},{"type":"compare","label":"Hammad, dissertation p. 307","text":"Compare Hammad’s rendering “four Qutbs [poles]”. The present edition translates the term as “principal axes” while retaining quṭb in the glossary. This is an editorial choice, not a textual variant."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"O02","part":"prelim","group":"Opening definition","title":"The five introductory questions","headingAr":"صدر الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:6","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/3/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%82%D9%87","ar":"ولنذكر في صدر الكتاب معنى أصول الفقه وحده وحقيقته أولا، ثم مرتبته ونسبته إلى العلوم ثانيا، ثم كيفية انشعابه إلى هذه المقدمة والأقطاب الأربعة ثالثا، ثم كيفية اندراج جميع أقسامه وتفاصيله تحت الأقطاب الأربعة رابعا، ثم وجه تعلقه بهذه المقدمة خامسا.","en":"At the outset, then, we shall first set out the meaning, definition, and reality of the principles of jurisprudence; second, their rank and relation to the other sciences; third, how this science branches into the introduction and the four axes; fourth, how all its parts and details fall under those axes; and fifth, how the introduction relates to the discipline itself.","terms":["usul","qutb"],"notes":[],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"O03","part":"prelim","group":"Opening definition","title":"From fiqh as understanding to fiqh as jurisprudence","headingAr":"بيان حد أصول الفقه","sourcePage":"I:6","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/3/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%82%D9%87","ar":"اعلم أنك لا تفهم معنى أصول الفقه ما لم تعرف أولا معنى الفقه، والفقه عبارة عن العلم والفهم في أصل الوضع، يقال فلان يفقه الخير والشر أي يعلمه ويفهمه، ولكن صار بعرف العلماء عبارة عن العلم بالأحكام الشرعية الثابتة لأفعال المكلفين خاصة.","en":"Understand that you cannot grasp the meaning of the principles of jurisprudence without first knowing what jurisprudence itself means. In its original linguistic use, fiqh signifies knowledge and understanding: to say that someone understands good and evil is to say that he knows and comprehends them. In scholars’ established usage, however, fiqh has come to mean specifically knowledge of the rulings of the revealed Law attaching to the acts of those subject to obligation.","terms":["usul","fiqh","hukm","taklif"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Fiqh is not “law” in every occurrence","text":"The movement from lexical “understanding” to the technical discipline is part of the argument; the two senses must not be collapsed."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"O04","part":"prelim","group":"Opening definition","title":"The distinct object of jurisprudence","headingAr":"بيان حد أصول الفقه","sourcePage":"I:6","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/3/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%82%D9%87","ar":"حتى لا يطلق بحكم العادة اسم الفقيه على متكلم وفلسفي ونحوي ومحدث ومفسر بل يختص بالعلماء بالأحكام الشرعية الثابتة للأفعال الإنسانية كالوجوب والحظر والإباحة والندب والكراهة وكون العقد صحيحا وفاسدا وباطلا وكون العبادة قضاء وأداء وأمثاله.","en":"Custom therefore does not call a theologian, philosopher, grammarian, hadith specialist, or exegete a jurist simply on account of that expertise. The term is reserved for those who know the legal rulings that attach to human actions: obligation, prohibition, permission, recommendation, and reprehensibility; whether a contract is sound, defective, or void; and whether an act of worship is performed at its prescribed time or made up afterward, and other such questions.","terms":["fiqh","hukm","taklif"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Ṣaḥīḥ, fāsid, bāṭil","text":"“Sound,” “defective,” and “void” are preserved as distinct terms. Their doctrinal relation cannot be assumed to be identical across the Shāfiʿī and Ḥanafī schools."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"O05","part":"prelim","group":"Opening definition","title":"Rational predicates versus legal qualifications","headingAr":"بيان حد أصول الفقه","sourcePage":"I:6","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/3/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%82%D9%87","ar":"ولا يخفى عليك أن للأفعال أحكاما عقلية أي مدركة بالعقل ككونها أعراضا وقائمة بالمحل ومخالفة للجوهر وكونها أكوانا حركة وسكونا وأمثالها، والعارف بذلك يسمى متكلما لا فقيها.","en":"It will not escape you that actions also have predicates knowable by reason: that they are accidents, subsist in a subject, differ from substance, or belong to the kinds of occurrence called motion and rest. Someone versed in these matters is called a theologian, not a jurist.","terms":["kalam","hukm","fiqh"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Aḥkām is not univocal","text":"In this sentence aḥkām can mean intelligible predicates or determinations. In the immediately following passage it means normative legal qualifications. A uniform English “rules” would obscure al-Ghazālī’s contrast."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"O06","part":"prelim","group":"Opening definition","title":"What uṣūl actually investigates","headingAr":"بيان حد أصول الفقه","sourcePage":"I:6","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/3/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%82%D9%87","ar":"وأما أحكامها من حيث إنها واجبة ومحظورة ومباحة ومكروهة ومندوب إليها فإنما يتولى الفقيه بيانها، فإذا فهمت هذا فافهم أن أصول الفقه عبارة عن أدلة هذه الأحكام وعن معرفة وجوه دلالتها على الأحكام من حيث الجملة لا من حيث التفصيل.","en":"The jurist, by contrast, explains the qualifications of acts insofar as they are obligatory, forbidden, permitted, disapproved, or recommended. Once you grasp this, understand that uṣūl al-fiqh concerns the proofs for such rulings, together with knowledge of the ways in which those proofs indicate rulings considered generally, not in the particular detail of each disputed case.","terms":["fiqh","usul","hukm","dalala"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Dalāla","text":"“Indication” is deliberate: a source’s evidentiary force is not equivalent to its merely containing an English statement of the ruling."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"O07","part":"prelim","group":"Opening definition","title":"General principles and particular cases","headingAr":"بيان حد أصول الفقه","sourcePage":"I:6","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/3/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%82%D9%87","ar":"وأما الأصول فلا يتعرض فيها لإحدى المسائل ولا على طريق ضرب المثال، بل يتعرض فيها لأصل الكتاب والسنة والإجماع ولشرائط صحتها وثبوتها ثم لوجوه دلالتها الجملية إما من حيث صيغتها أو مفهوم لفظها أو مجرى لفظها أو معقول لفظها وهو القياس من غير أن يتعرض فيها لمسألة خاصة.","en":"As for the principles, they do not treat a specific legal problem as their subject, even for the sake of expounding its details. Rather, they investigate the Qurʾān, the Sunna, and consensus as foundational proofs, the conditions under which each is established and sound, and the general modes of their indication: by the form of an expression, by what is understood from its wording, by the course of its wording, or by its intelligible sense—the last being analogy—without examining any particular disputed issue.","terms":["usul","ijma","qiyas","dalala"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"“Without an individual issue” does not abolish examples","text":"This passage distinguishes subject matter and method. Al-Ghazālī uses examples throughout the book but refuses to make a particular fiqh ruling the object of uṣūl."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"O08","part":"prelim","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"What counts as uṣūl","headingAr":"صدر الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:6","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/3/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%82%D9%87","ar":"فأصول الفقه عبارة عن أدلة هذه الأحكام وعن معرفة وجوه دلالتها على الأحكام من حيث الجملة لا من حيث التفصيل","en":"Uṣūl al-fiqh consists of the proofs of these rulings, together with knowledge of the general ways in which those proofs indicate rulings—not the examination of every detailed case.","terms":["usul","dalala","hukm"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"General versus particular","text":"Min ḥayth al-jumla is a methodological restriction on the discipline, not a claim that detailed rulings are irrelevant to its application."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"O09","part":"prelim","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"The three established textual sources","headingAr":"صدر الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:6","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/3/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%82%D9%87","ar":"وقد عرفت من هذا أن أدلة الأحكام الكتاب والسنة والإجماع","en":"You can now see that the proofs of rulings are the Book, the Sunna, and consensus.","terms":["hukm","ijma","hujjiyya"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Not a denial of juristic reasoning","text":"The fourth discussion, inference from these proofs, is developed as a method of deriving conclusions, not necessarily as a coequal new revelatory source."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"O10","part":"prelim","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"Religious sciences include an inward science","headingAr":"صدر الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:7","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/4/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%AA%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D9%88%D9%86%D8%B3%D8%A8%D8%AA%D9%87-%D8%A5%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85","ar":"وإلى دينية كالكلام والفقه وأصوله وعلم الحديث وعلم التفسير وعلم الباطن، أعني علم القلب وتطهيره عن الأخلاق الذميمة، وكل واحد من العقلية والدينية ينقسم إلى كلية وجزئية.","en":"Other sciences are religious: theology, jurisprudence and its principles, the science of prophetic reports, Qurʾānic interpretation, and the inward science—by which I mean knowledge of the heart and its purification from blameworthy dispositions. Both the rational and the religious sciences divide into universal and particular disciplines.","terms":["kalam","fiqh","usul","kulli","ilm_batin"],"notes":[{"type":"context","label":"Not a simple worldly/spiritual opposition","text":"The inward discipline is expressly included in this taxonomy. The hierarchy developed in the next paragraph concerns the generality of the subject investigated, not the worth of a life devoted to the science."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"O11","part":"prelim","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"The disciplinary domain of theology and law","headingAr":"صدر الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:7","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/4/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%AA%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D9%88%D9%86%D8%B3%D8%A8%D8%AA%D9%87-%D8%A5%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85","ar":"فالعلم الكلي من العلوم الدينية هو الكلام وسائر العلوم من الفقه وأصوله والحديث والتفسير علوم جزئية، لأن المفسر لا ينظر إلا في معنى الكتاب خاصة، والمحدث لا ينظر إلا في طريق ثبوت الحديث خاصة، والفقيه لا ينظر إلا في أحكام أفعال المكلفين خاصة، والأصولي لا ينظر إلا في أدلة الأحكام الشرعية خاصة، والمتكلم هو الذي ينظر في أعم الأشياء وهو الموجود.","en":"Among the religious sciences, theology is the universal discipline, while jurisprudence, its principles, reports, and exegesis are particular disciplines. The exegete investigates the meaning of the Book as such; the traditionist examines the means by which a report is established; the jurist examines rulings governing the acts of those under legal obligation; the uṣūlī examines the evidence for legal rulings; and the theologian investigates the most general object of all, namely what exists.","terms":["kalam","fiqh","usul","hukm","kulli"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Universality means breadth of subject","text":"Do not translate kullī as if Ghazālī were calling kalām universally true and fiqh merely local. His criterion here is extension of the field of inquiry."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"A01","part":"prelim","group":"Relation to the other sciences","title":"Rational and religious sciences","headingAr":"بيان مرتبة هذا العلم ونسبته إلى العلوم","sourcePage":"I:7","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/4/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%AA%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D9%88%D9%86%D8%B3%D8%A8%D8%AA%D9%87-%D8%A5%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85","ar":"اعلم أن العلوم تنقسم إلى عقلية كالطب والحساب والهندسة وليس ذلك من غرضنا، وإلى دينية كالكلام والفقه وأصوله وعلم الحديث وعلم التفسير وعلم الباطن أعني علم القلب وتطهيره عن الأخلاق الذميمة وكل واحد من العقلية والدينية ينقسم إلى كلية وجزئية.","en":"Know that the sciences divide into rational disciplines, such as medicine, arithmetic, and geometry—which do not concern us here—and religious disciplines, such as theology, jurisprudence and its principles, the science of hadith, Qurʾānic exegesis, and the inward science, by which I mean the science of the heart and its purification from blameworthy dispositions. Both rational and religious sciences may be divided into universal and particular.","terms":["kalam","fiqh","usul","kulli"],"notes":[{"type":"crosswork","label":"Connection to the Iḥyāʾ","text":"The science of the heart is explicitly included in this classification. Comparison with the Iḥyāʾ on knowledge and cultivation is warranted, but no terminological identity has been presumed."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"A02","part":"prelim","group":"Relation to the other sciences","title":"Why kalām is universal here","headingAr":"بيان مرتبة هذا العلم ونسبته إلى العلوم","sourcePage":"I:7","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/4/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%AA%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D9%88%D9%86%D8%B3%D8%A8%D8%AA%D9%87-%D8%A5%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85","ar":"فالعلم الكلي من العلوم الدينية هو الكلام وسائر العلوم من الفقه وأصوله والحديث والتفسير علوم جزئية، لأن المفسر لا ينظر إلا في معنى الكتاب خاصة والمحدث لا ينظر إلا في طريق ثبوت الحديث خاصة والفقيه لا ينظر إلا في أحكام أفعال المكلفين خاصة والأصولي لا ينظر إلا في أدلة الأحكام الشرعية خاصة.","en":"Among the religious sciences it is kalām that is universal. The others—jurisprudence and its principles, hadith, and exegesis—are particular: the exegete investigates the meaning of the Book; the hadith scholar, the means by which a report is established; the jurist, the rulings concerning the acts of accountable persons; and the uṣūl specialist, the evidence for those legal rulings.","terms":["kalam","kulli","fiqh","hukm","usul"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Universal does not mean self-sufficient","text":"His claim concerns the level of generality of kalām’s object, not that every jurist is personally required to prove every theological premise."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"F01","part":"prelim","group":"Four axes","title":"From evidence to derivation","headingAr":"بيان كيفية دورانه على الأقطاب الأربعة","sourcePage":"I:8","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/5/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D9%83%D9%8A%D9%81%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A7%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B1%D8%A8%D8%B9%D8%A9","ar":"اعلم أنك إذا فهمت أن نظر الأصولي في وجوه دلالة الأدلة السمعية على الأحكام الشرعية لم يخف عليك أن المقصود معرفة كيفية اقتباس الأحكام من الأدلة، فوجب النظر في الأحكام ثم في الأدلة وأقسامها ثم في كيفية اقتباس الأحكام من الأدلة ثم في صفات المقتبس الذي له أن يقتبس الأحكام.","en":"Once you understand that the theorist of uṣūl investigates how transmitted proofs indicate legal rulings, you will see that the aim is to know how rulings are drawn from evidence. We must therefore consider the rulings, then the proofs and their classes, then the means of drawing rulings from them, and finally the qualities of the person entitled to draw them.","terms":["usul","hukm","dalala","ijtihad"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Iqtibās","text":"“Draw from” tracks iqtibās, which leads into the sustained fruit-and-harvest image. It does not posit an extra, independent source of law."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"F02","part":"prelim","group":"Four axes","title":"Fruit, productive source, method, and harvester","headingAr":"بيان كيفية دورانه على الأقطاب الأربعة","sourcePage":"I:8","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/5/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D9%83%D9%8A%D9%81%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A7%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B1%D8%A8%D8%B9%D8%A9","ar":"فإن الأحكام ثمرات وكل ثمرة فلها صفة وحقيقة في نفسها ولها مثمر ومستثمر وطريق في الاستثمار، والثمرة هي الأحكام أعني الوجوب والحظر والندب والكراهة والإباحة والحسن والقبح والقضاء والأداء والصحة والفساد وغيرها والمثمر هي الأدلة وهي ثلاثة الكتاب والسنة والإجماع فقط.","en":"Legal rulings are fruits. Every fruit has a character and reality of its own, something that brings it forth, someone who harvests it, and a way of harvesting it. The fruits are the rulings—obligation, prohibition, recommendation, disapproval, permission, goodness and badness, performance and restitution, validity and invalidity, and the rest. The things that bear fruit are the proofs, which are three: the Book, the Sunna, and consensus.","terms":["hukm","qutb","ijma"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Fruit is an operative analytical image","text":"The distinction between what counts as evidence and the method of drawing from evidence becomes the organizing device for this entire book."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"F03","part":"prelim","group":"Four axes","title":"The four modes of indication","headingAr":"بيان كيفية دورانه على الأقطاب الأربعة","sourcePage":"I:8","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/5/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D9%83%D9%8A%D9%81%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A7%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B1%D8%A8%D8%B9%D8%A9","ar":"وطرق الاستثمار هي وجوه دلالة الأدلة وهي أربعة، إذ الأقوال إما أن تدل على الشيء بصيغتها ومنظومها أو بفحواها ومفهومها وباقتضائها وضرورتها أو بمعقولها ومعناها المستنبط منها.","en":"The methods of harvesting are the modes in which proofs indicate their conclusions. These are four: an utterance may indicate something by its articulated form and explicit wording; by its import and understood sense; by what its necessary implications require; or by its intelligible meaning as drawn from it.","terms":["dalala","qiyas"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Technical modes remain distinct","text":"The pairing and counting require attention to al-Ghazālī’s own subsequent articulation, not a one-to-one equation with modern literal, contextual, or purposive methods."},{"type":"translation-comparison","label":"With Hammad (1987), printed p. 315–316","text":"Hammad foregrounds the explicit, implicit and rationally entailed types of indication. The new version keeps the metaphor of extracting the fruit and preserves the distinction between the utterance’s form, its purport, what it necessarily implies, and the inference that joins premises. The terms require continued mapping to his later chapters on dalāla."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"F04","part":"prelim","group":"Four axes","title":"The jurist and the four axes","headingAr":"بيان كيفية دورانه على الأقطاب الأربعة","sourcePage":"I:8","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/5/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D9%83%D9%8A%D9%81%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A7%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B1%D8%A8%D8%B9%D8%A9","ar":"والمستثمر هو المجتهد ولا بد من معرفة صفاته وشروطه وأحكامه، فإذا جملة الأصول تدور على أربعة أقطاب: القطب الأول في الأحكام والبداءة بها أولى لأنها الثمرة المطلوبة. القطب الثاني في الأدلة وهي الكتاب والسنة والإجماع. القطب الثالث في طريق الاستثمار وهو وجوه دلالة الأدلة. القطب الرابع في المستثمر وهو المجتهد الذي يحكم بظنه، ويقابله المقلد الذي يلزمه اتباعه.","en":"The harvester is the mujtahid; his qualifications, conditions, and rulings must therefore be known. The entire discipline revolves around four principal axes: first, the rulings, with which we begin because they are the fruits sought; second, the proofs—the Book, the Sunna, and consensus; third, the process of harvesting, namely the modes through which proofs indicate; and fourth, the harvester himself, the mujtahid, who renders judgment on the basis of his considered probability, opposite whom stands the follower who is bound to follow him.","terms":["ijtihad","zann","qutb","hukm","ijma","dalala"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Explicit ẓann at the architectural level","text":"The jurist “judges by his ẓann” is al-Ghazālī’s phrase at the overview, well before the long debate over divergent ijtihād. Ẓann is not merely an exceptional failure of proof."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"F05","part":"prelim","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Interpretation has several routes","headingAr":"صدر الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:8","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/5/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D9%83%D9%8A%D9%81%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A7%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B1%D8%A8%D8%B9%D8%A9","ar":"وطرق الاستثمار هي وجوه دلالة الأدلة وهي أربعة","en":"The methods of drawing out legal rulings are the modes in which the proofs signify, and these modes are four.","terms":["dalala","qutb","hukm"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Istithmār is deliberate metaphor","text":"“Drawing out” retains the author’s sustained imagery of the fruit, its producer, the cultivator, and the manner of harvesting."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"F06","part":"prelim","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"The inquirer and the follower","headingAr":"صدر الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:8","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/5/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D9%83%D9%8A%D9%81%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A7%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B1%D8%A8%D8%B9%D8%A9","ar":"القطب الرابع في المستثمر وهو المجتهد الذي يحكم بظنه، ويقابله المقلد الذي يلزمه اتباعه","en":"The fourth principal division concerns the one who draws out the fruit: the mujtahid, who reaches a ruling through predominant judgment. Opposite him stands the follower, whose task is to follow that jurist.","terms":["ijtihad","zann","qutb"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Zann is not knowledge by demonstration","text":"This fourth place is essential for connecting the results of probable argument to the qualified practitioner authorized to act on them."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"F07","part":"prelim","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Rules as legal addresses, not properties of acts","headingAr":"صدر الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:8","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/6/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D9%83%D9%8A%D9%81%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%AF%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%AC-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B4%D8%B9%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AB%D9%8A%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%82%D9%87-%D8%AA%D8%AD%D8%AA-%D9%87%D8%B0%D9%87-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A7%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B1%D8%A8%D8%B9%D8%A9","ar":"فيتبين أنه عبارة عن خطاب الشرع وليس وصفا للفعل ولا حسن ولا قبح ولا مدخل للعقل فيه ولا حكم قبل ورود الشرع","en":"The ruling is shown to be an address of revealed law, not an intrinsic description of the act—not simply its being good or bad. On this account reason does not itself establish such a legal ruling before the revealed address arrives.","terms":["hukm","khitab"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Theological thesis","text":"Here al-Ghazālī outlines an Ashʿarī legal-theological argument; it should not be flattened into the claim that actions have no intelligible natural features."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"S09","part":"prelim","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"Why the definitions of knowledge and evidence arose","headingAr":"صدر الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:10","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/7/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%82%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%A9-%D9%88%D9%88%D8%AC%D9%87-%D8%AA%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%82-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A8%D9%87%D8%A7","ar":"اعلم أنه لما رجع حد أصول الفقه إلى معرفة أدلة الأحكام اشتمل الحد على ثلاثة ألفاظ: المعرفة والدليل والحكم، فقالوا: إذا لم يكن بد من معرفة الحكم حتى كان معرفته أحد الأقطاب الأربعة فلا بد أيضا من معرفة الدليل ومعرفة المعرفة، أعني العلم.","en":"Know that once the definition of uṣūl al-fiqh had been reduced to knowing the proofs of rulings, it comprised three terms: knowing, proof, and ruling. Scholars therefore said: if knowledge of a ruling is indispensable—so indispensable that it constitutes one of the four principal divisions—then it is equally necessary to know what a proof is and what knowing itself means, that is, to investigate knowledge.","terms":["usul","hujjiyya","hukm","kulli"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Definitional clarification versus independent proof","text":"Al-Ghazālī is reporting the development of a curriculum from terms contained in its definition, not conceding that a full epistemology belongs intrinsically to uṣūl."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"S10","part":"prelim","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"The danger of importing another discipline wholesale","headingAr":"صدر الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:10","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/7/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%82%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%A9-%D9%88%D9%88%D8%AC%D9%87-%D8%AA%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%82-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A8%D9%87%D8%A7","ar":"وذلك مجاوزة لحد هذا العلم وخلط له بالكلام، وإنما أكثر فيه المتكلمون من الأصوليين لغلبة الكلام على طبائعهم فحملهم حب صناعتهم على خلطه بهذه الصنعة، كما حمل حب اللغة والنحو بعض الأصوليين على مزج جملة من النحو بالأصول.","en":"This exceeds the proper bounds of the discipline and mixes it with theology. The theologians among the uṣūlīs expanded it in that direction because theological debate had come to dominate their intellectual habits: love of their own craft led them to mix it with this one, just as attachment to language and grammar led some uṣūlīs to import a considerable quantity of grammar into legal theory.","terms":["usul","kalam"],"notes":[{"type":"context","label":"An internal criticism of encyclopedic uṣūl","text":"The author does not simply reject rational method. He distinguishes supplying concepts needed by an inquiry from pursuing external disputes within its pages."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"S11","part":"prelim","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"The limit of legitimate prolegomena","headingAr":"صدر الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:10","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/7/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%82%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%A9-%D9%88%D9%88%D8%AC%D9%87-%D8%AA%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%82-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A8%D9%87%D8%A7","ar":"وأما معرفة حجية الإجماع وحجية القياس فذلك من خاصية أصول الفقه، فذكر حجية العلم والنظر على منكريه استجرار الأصول إلى الفروع.","en":"Establishing the probative authority of consensus and of analogy is a task proper to uṣūl al-fiqh. By contrast, arguing for the authority of knowledge and reasoning against those who deny them drags the science of principles into subjects that are not its own.","terms":["usul","ijma","qiyas","hujjiyya"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Evidence within a discipline versus its presuppositions","text":"This passage helps distinguish legal evidentiary authority from general epistemic justification, and matters for comparisons with Aristotelian demonstrative method."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"S04","part":"prelim","title":"The proper boundary of legal theory","headingAr":"بيان المقدمة ووجه تعلق الأصول بها","sourcePage":"I:10","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/7/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%82%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%A9-%D9%88%D9%88%D8%AC%D9%87-%D8%AA%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%82-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A8%D9%87%D8%A7","ar":"وأما معرفة حجية الإجماع وحجية القياس فذلك من خاصية أصول الفقه","en":"But determining the probative authority of consensus and of analogy belongs specifically to the principles of jurisprudence.","terms":["ijma","qiyas","hujjiyya"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Authority, not mere acquaintance","text":"Ḥujjiyya is the status of furnishing an authoritative ground for inference, not simply whether consensus or analogy exists. Al-Ghazālī distinguishes that question from general epistemology."}],"group":"Selected loci","coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L14","part":"logic","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"Single concepts cannot yet be true or false","headingAr":"مقدمة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:11","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%B5%D8%B1-%D9%85%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%83-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%86%D8%B8%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86","ar":"وأما الأول فيستحيل فيه التصديق والتكذيب إذ لا يتطرق التصديق إلا إلى خبر، وأقل ما يتركب منه جزءان مفردان وصف وموصوف، فإذا نسب الوصف إلى الموصوف بنفي أو إثبات صدق أو كذب.","en":"The first kind cannot admit assent or rejection as true or false, because such assessment is possible only with a proposition. At the least, a proposition is composed of two single elements, something described and a description. Only when the description is predicated of the subject, affirmatively or negatively, can the resulting statement be true or false.","terms":["tasawwur","tasdiq"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Concept and judgement","text":"The minimal structure here is logical predication. It should not be confused with a legal judgement (ḥukm sharʿī), whose validity involves the authority of the revealed address."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L15","part":"logic","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"Every assent presupposes conceptual grasp","headingAr":"مقدمة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:11","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%B5%D8%B1-%D9%85%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%83-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%86%D8%B8%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86","ar":"وكل علم تطرق إليه تصديق فمن ضرورته أن يتقدم عليه معرفتان أي تصوران، فإن من لا يعرف المفرد كيف يعلم المركب، ومن لا يفهم معنى العالم ومعنى الحادث كيف يعلم أن العالم حادث.","en":"Every cognition that admits assent must necessarily be preceded by two acts of acquaintance—two conceptions. For how could someone know a compound proposition who does not grasp its component notions? How could one know that the world is originated without first understanding what “world” and “originated” mean?","terms":["tasawwur","tasdiq"],"notes":[{"type":"crosswork","label":"Near-verbal agreement with Miḥakk al-Naẓar","text":"The sequence and examples are closely paralleled in Ghazālī’s logical manual. This supports a direct comparative text, not a generic equation of law and demonstration."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L16","part":"logic","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"Primary and sought concepts","headingAr":"مقدمة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:11","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%B5%D8%B1-%D9%85%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%83-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%86%D8%B8%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86","ar":"ومعرفة المفردات قسمان: أولي وهو الذي لا يطلب بالبحث وهو الذي يرتسم معناه في النفس من غير بحث وطلب كلفظ الوجود والشيء وككثير من المحسوسات، ومطلوب وهو الذي يدل اسمه منه على أمر جملي غير مفصل ولا مفسر فيطلب تفسيره بالحد، وكذلك العلم ينقسم إلى أولي كالضروريات وإلى مطلوب كالنظريات.","en":"Knowledge of individual notions divides in two. Some is primary: its meaning is formed in the mind without investigation, as with “existence,” “thing,” and many sensory objects. Other notions are sought: their names point to something grasped only generally and without articulation, so an account of them is sought by definition. Cognition of propositions is likewise either primary, as in necessary truths, or something to be sought, as in results of theoretical inquiry.","terms":["awwaliyat","hadd","tasawwur","burhan"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"No infinite regress of definitions","text":"Al-Ghazālī distinguishes immediately evident intelligibles from contents that require clarification. The contrast underlies his later discussion of what can be demonstrated and what must be presupposed."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L17","part":"logic","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"Definition and demonstration have different tasks","headingAr":"مقدمة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:11","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%B5%D8%B1-%D9%85%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%83-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%86%D8%B8%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86","ar":"والمطلوب من المعرفة لا يقتنص إلا بالحد، والمطلوب من العلم الذي يتطرق إليه التصديق والتكذيب لا يقتنص إلا بالبرهان، فالبرهان والحد هو الآلة التي بها يقتنص سائر العلوم المطلوبة.","en":"What is sought at the level of conceptual acquaintance can be attained only by definition; what is sought in propositional cognition—the kind open to affirmation or denial—can be attained only by demonstration. Thus definition and demonstration are the instruments through which the remaining objects of theoretical knowledge are acquired.","terms":["hadd","burhan","tasawwur","tasdiq"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Not two interchangeable kinds of argument","text":"Definition makes intelligible what a thing is meant to be; demonstration determines whether a propositional claim follows. Later legal evidence may be probabilistic without making every syllogism demonstrative."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L01","part":"logic","group":"Logical introduction","title":"The universal prolegomenon","headingAr":"مقدمة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:11","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%B5%D8%B1-%D9%85%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%83-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%86%D8%B8%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86","ar":"نذكر في هذه المقدمة مدارك العقول وانحصارها في الحد والبرهان، ونذكر شرط الحد الحقيقي وشرط البرهان الحقيقي وأقسامهما على منهاج أوجز مما ذكرناه في كتاب محك النظر وكتاب معيار العلم.","en":"In this introduction we set out the ways by which intellects apprehend matters, and their delimitation to definition and demonstration. We shall describe the conditions of a genuine definition and a genuine demonstration, together with their varieties, more concisely than we have done in Miḥakk al-Naẓar and Miʿyār al-ʿIlm.","terms":["hadd","burhan","yaqin"],"notes":[{"type":"crosswork","label":"Explicit links: Miḥakk and Miʿyār","text":"Both titles appear in the Arabic passage. The link is direct and authorial, not simply topical."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L02","part":"logic","group":"Logical introduction","title":"Why logic is not the exclusive property of uṣūl","headingAr":"مقدمة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:11","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%B5%D8%B1-%D9%85%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%83-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%86%D8%B8%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86","ar":"وليست هذه المقدمة من جملة علم الأصول ولا من مقدماته الخاصة به، بل هي مقدمة العلوم كلها، ومن لا يحيط بها فلا ثقة له بعلومه أصلا، فمن شاء أن لا يكتب هذه المقدمة فليبدأ بالكتاب من القطب الأول.","en":"This introduction is not part of the science of legal principles, nor is it a preliminary peculiar to it; rather, it is a prolegomenon to all the sciences. Anyone who has not mastered it cannot be trusted in his sciences at all. Whoever wishes to omit this introduction may therefore begin with the first axis of the book.","terms":["usul","hadd","burhan"],"notes":[{"type":"compare","label":"Compare treatment in Hammad (pending collation)","text":"The earlier English Volume I translator’s treatment of this introduction requires a direct comparison with the previously uploaded copy; the present edition independently translates the Arabic passage."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L03","part":"logic","group":"Logical introduction","title":"Two kinds of apprehension","headingAr":"بيان حصر مدارك العلوم النظرية في الحد والبرهان","sourcePage":"I:11","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%B5%D8%B1-%D9%85%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%83-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%86%D8%B8%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86","ar":"اعلم أن إدراك الأمور على ضربين: الأول إدراك الذوات المفردة كعلمك بمعنى الجسم والحركة والعالم والحادث والقديم، وسائر ما يدل عليه بالأسامي المفردة. الثاني إدراك نسبة هذه المفردات بعضها إلى بعض بالنفي أو الإثبات.","en":"Know that apprehension has two kinds. The first is the apprehension of individual notions, such as grasping the meanings of body, motion, the world, the originated, and the eternal, and of other things denoted by single names. The second is apprehending a relation between these notions, whether by affirmation or denial.","terms":["hadd","burhan"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Taṣawwur and taṣdīq","text":"The first kind is associated with conception, the second with assent or judgment. This pair informs the distinction between definition and demonstrative proof."},{"type":"source","label":"Source location","text":"The passage is found within the opening logical introduction. This is an online-text location, not an independently collated manuscript or print-variant reading."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L06","part":"logic","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"The knowledge of a single meaning","headingAr":"مقدمة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:11","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D9%85%D9%82%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","ar":"إدراك الذوات المفردة كعلمك بمعنى الجسم والحركة والعالم والحادث والقديم","en":"The first sort of apprehension concerns single things or meanings: for example, one grasps what body, movement, world, originated, and eternal signify.","terms":["hadd","burhan"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Mufrad here means a conceptual unit","text":"It should not simply be translated “simple substance”; this is an intentional/logical distinction."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L07","part":"logic","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"The knowledge of an affirmative or negative relation","headingAr":"مقدمة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:11","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D9%85%D9%82%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","ar":"الثاني: إدراك نسبة هذه المفردات بعضها إلى بعض بالنفي أو الإثبات","en":"The second sort of apprehension consists in grasping a relation among these units by affirming or denying one of another.","terms":["dalala"],"notes":[{"type":"crosswork","label":"Verbal parallel in Miḥakk al-Naẓar","text":"The structure of this passage closely reproduces the treatment of singular terms and propositions in al-Ghazālī’s earlier logical manual."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L08","part":"logic","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Truth or falsity requires predication","headingAr":"مقدمة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:11","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D9%85%D9%82%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","ar":"والضرب الأخير هو الذي يتطرق إليه التصديق والتكذيب، وأما الأول فيستحيل فيه التصديق والتكذيب","en":"It is this latter sort of apprehension that admits assent or denial. The first sort, considered by itself, cannot be affirmed as true or rejected as false.","terms":["burhan","yaqin"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Not the same as probable assent","text":"The first distinction is between concepts and propositions, whereas the subsequent distinction of yaqīn and ẓann concerns degrees or kinds of assent to propositions."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L09","part":"logic","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Conception and assent","headingAr":"مقدمة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:11","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D9%85%D9%82%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","ar":"وقد سمى المنطقيون معرفة المفردات تصورا ومعرفة النسبة الخبرية بينهما تصديقا فقالوا: العلم إما تصور وإما تصديق","en":"The logicians call grasping the single terms *conception* (taṣawwur), and apprehending their asserted relation *assent* (taṣdīq). In their terminology cognition is thus either conception or assent.","terms":["burhan","hadd","yaqin"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Preserving the two logical operations","text":"This is not a division between literal and metaphorical knowledge, nor between religious assent and scientific conception."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L10","part":"logic","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"The two instruments of inquiry","headingAr":"مقدمة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:11","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D9%85%D9%82%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","ar":"والمطلوب من المعرفة لا يقتنص إلا بالحد، والمطلوب من العلم الذي يتطرق إليه التصديق والتكذيب لا يقتنص إلا بالبرهان","en":"A concept whose meaning must be sought is captured through a definition; a proposition to which truth and falsehood apply is established through demonstration.","terms":["hadd","burhan"],"notes":[{"type":"crosswork","label":"Direct logical intertext","text":"The exposition in Miḥakk al-Naẓar closely matches both the division and its pairing with definition and demonstration."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L11","part":"logic","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"What is prior and what is sought","headingAr":"مقدمة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:11","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D9%85%D9%82%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","ar":"ومعرفة المفردات قسمان: أولي وهو الذي لا يطلب بالبحث؛ ومطلوب وهو الذي يدل اسمه منه على أمر جملي غير مفصل","en":"Conception of individual meanings is of two kinds. One is primary and does not need to be sought by investigation; the other begins from a general, unarticulated grasp and requires the meaning to be made explicit.","terms":["hadd"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Awwalī is epistemic","text":"Primary is not “chronologically first” in every individual’s history; it marks what is not established through another definition."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L12","part":"logic","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Demonstration depends on the premises","headingAr":"مقدمة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:31","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/29/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%8A-%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%AF%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86","ar":"والاعتقاد الجزم ينحصر في سبعة أقسام: الأول الأوليات","en":"Grounds of firm assent fall into seven classes. The first consists of primary truths grasped immediately by the intellect.","terms":["burhan","yaqin"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Matter as well as form","text":"A formally valid syllogism does not by itself ensure demonstrated certainty; the status of its premises must also be established."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L13","part":"logic","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"The law of contradiction as a primary truth","headingAr":"مقدمة الكتاب","sourcePage":"I:31","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/29/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%8A-%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%AF%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86","ar":"مثل علم الإنسان بوجود نفسه وبأن الواحد لا يكون قديما حادثا وأن النقيضين إذا صدق أحدهما كذب الآخر","en":"Examples include a person’s awareness of his own existence, the impossibility of one thing being both eternal and originated, and the impossibility of both contradictories being true.","terms":["yaqin","burhan"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Al-ḍiddān vs al-naqīḍān","text":"Keep the contrast between contraries and contradictories distinct where al-Ghazālī does; this short excerpt specifically names contradiction."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"S06","part":"logic","title":"What demonstration consists in","headingAr":"الفصل الأول في صورة البرهان","sourcePage":"I:31","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/25/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%88%D9%84-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%B5%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86","ar":"والبرهان عبارة عن مقدمتين معلومتين تؤلف تأليفا مخصوصا بشرط مخصوص، فيتولد بينهما نتيجة","en":"Demonstration consists in two known premises combined in a determinate arrangement, subject to a determinate condition, from which a conclusion follows.","terms":["burhan","hadd","qiyas"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"“Known premises” and formal consequence","text":"This describes the formal configuration of an inference; the degree of certainty delivered also depends on the epistemic standing of its premises, treated separately in the material of demonstration."}],"group":"Additional logical loci","coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L04","part":"logic","group":"Logical introduction","title":"Demonstrative, dialectical, and juristic premises","headingAr":"الفصل الأول في صورة البرهان","sourcePage":"I:31","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/25/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%88%D9%84-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%B5%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86","ar":"فإن كانت المقدمات قطعية سميناها برهانا وإن كانت مسلمة سميناها قياسا جدليا وإن كانت مظنونة سميناها قياسا فقهيا، وسيأتي الفرق بين اليقين والظن.","en":"If the premises are conclusive, we call the inference a demonstration; if they are accepted as granted, we call it a dialectical syllogism; and if they are held on probable grounds, we call it a juristic inference. The difference between certainty and probable judgment will be explained later.","terms":["burhan","yaqin","zann","qiyas"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Burhān in strict and extended senses","text":"This passage explicitly distinguishes the epistemic force of premises. It limits the sense of burhān as strict proof and uses qiyās across logical and juridical domains."},{"type":"context","label":"A terminological boundary","text":"“Demonstration” is strictly tied to certainty of premises in its technical sense. Juristic inference can instead be sound in a practical, defeasible manner."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"L05","part":"logic","group":"Logical introduction","title":"A direct reference to Asās al-Qiyās","headingAr":"الفصل الأول في صورة البرهان","sourcePage":"I:31","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/25/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%88%D9%84-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%B5%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86","ar":"وقد ذكرنا في كتاب أساس القياس أن تسمية هذا قياسا تجوز فإن حاصله راجع إلى ازدواج خصوص تحت عموم.","en":"We explained in Asās al-Qiyās that calling this “analogy” is a figurative extension: in substance, it consists in joining a particular instance to a general proposition.","terms":["qiyas","burhan"],"notes":[{"type":"crosswork","label":"Asās al-Qiyās explicitly named","text":"This is a direct authorial link. It is especially valuable for preventing the legal use of qiyās from being flattened into the structure of a syllogism or vice versa."}],"coverage":"continuous excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"H01","part":"hukm","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"The ruling is a divine legal address","headingAr":"القطب الأول في الأحكام","sourcePage":"I:45","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/37/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%88%D9%84-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D9%88%D9%87%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D9%83%D9%85-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%85-%D9%81%D9%8A%D9%87-%D9%8A%D9%86%D9%82%D8%B3%D9%85-%D8%A5%D9%84%D9%89-%D9%81%D9%86%D9%88%D9%86-%D8%A3%D8%B1%D8%A8%D8%B9%D8%A9","ar":"أما التمهيد فهو أن الحكم عندنا عبارة عن خطاب الشرع إذا تعلق بأفعال المكلفين","en":"As a preliminary, what we call a legal ruling is the address of the revealed Law insofar as it concerns the acts of those answerable to it.","terms":["hukm","khitab","taklif"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Theory of the address","text":"What is ontologically primary is the authoritative address; the predicates “obligatory” and “prohibited” qualify acts in relation to this address."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"H02","part":"hukm","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Three elementary responses","headingAr":"القطب الأول في الأحكام","sourcePage":"I:45","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/37/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%88%D9%84-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D9%88%D9%87%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D9%83%D9%85-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%85-%D9%81%D9%8A%D9%87-%D9%8A%D9%86%D9%82%D8%B3%D9%85-%D8%A5%D9%84%D9%89-%D9%81%D9%86%D9%88%D9%86-%D8%A3%D8%B1%D8%A8%D8%B9%D8%A9","ar":"فالحرام هو المقول فيه اتركوه ولا تفعلوه، والواجب هو المقول فيه افعلوه ولا تتركوه","en":"What is prohibited is that concerning which the address says “Leave it; do not do it.” What is obligatory is that concerning which it says “Do it; do not leave it.”","terms":["hukm","taklif"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Commands in direct speech","text":"The imperatives are illustrations of the force of the address, not a claim that every command or prohibition appears in precisely this verbal formula."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"H03","part":"hukm","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Optional choice is also expressed by address","headingAr":"القطب الأول في الأحكام","sourcePage":"I:45","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/37/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%88%D9%84-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D9%88%D9%87%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D9%83%D9%85-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%85-%D9%81%D9%8A%D9%87-%D9%8A%D9%86%D9%82%D8%B3%D9%85-%D8%A5%D9%84%D9%89-%D9%81%D9%86%D9%88%D9%86-%D8%A3%D8%B1%D8%A8%D8%B9%D8%A9","ar":"والمباح هو المقول فيه إن شئتم فافعلوه وإن شئتم فاتركوه","en":"What is permitted is that of which it is said, “If you wish, do it; and if you wish, leave it.”","terms":["hukm","taklif"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Permission and original nonliability","text":"A later discussion distinguishes a permissive legal address from the mere absence of a legal pronouncement."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"H06","part":"hukm","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Why original permissibility is not a prior enacted permission","headingAr":"القطب الأول في الأحكام","sourcePage":"I:52","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/39/%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%A3%D9%84%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%81%D8%B9%D8%A7%D9%84-%D9%82%D8%A8%D9%84-%D9%88%D8%B1%D9%88%D8%AF-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B4%D8%B1%D8%B9-%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A5%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%AD%D8%A9","ar":"المباح يستدعي مبيحا كما يستدعي العلم والذكر ذاكرا وعالما","en":"Permissibility presupposes one who permits, just as knowing and remembering presuppose a knower and one who remembers.","terms":["hukm","khitab"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Do not confuse absence of restriction with declared permissibility","text":"Al-Ghazālī’s distinction concerns whether a normative address has occurred; he can still admit the absence of liability without calling it a prior legal dispensation."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"H04","part":"hukm","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"The five evaluative categories","headingAr":"القطب الأول في الأحكام","sourcePage":"I:53","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/40/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%8A-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A3%D9%82%D8%B3%D8%A7%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AD%D9%83%D8%A7%D9%85","ar":"أقسام الأحكام الثابتة لأفعال المكلفين خمسة: الواجب والمحظور والمباح والمندوب والمكروه","en":"There are five classes of legal rulings concerning the acts of accountable persons: obligatory, prohibited, permitted, recommended, and reprehensible.","terms":["hukm","taklif"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"The evaluative register","text":"The obligatory and recommended are not interchangeable, nor do prohibited and reprehensible have the same practical force."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"H05","part":"hukm","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Obligation and recommendation distinguished","headingAr":"القطب الأول في الأحكام","sourcePage":"I:53","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/40/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%8A-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A3%D9%82%D8%B3%D8%A7%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AD%D9%83%D8%A7%D9%85","ar":"فإن ورد باقتضاء الفعل فهو أمر فإما أن يقترن به الإشعار بعقاب على الترك فيكون واجبا أو لا يقترن فيكون ندبا","en":"When the address demands the performance of an act, it is a command. If it carries notice of punishment for omission, the act is obligatory; if not, it is recommended.","terms":["hukm","taklif"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Normative force is not bare linguistic form","text":"The category depends upon the demand together with its legal consequence, not merely the occurrence of an imperative grammatical form."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"S08","part":"hukm","title":"Ruling and the divine address","headingAr":"الفن الثالث من القطب الأول في أركان الحكم","sourcePage":"I:67","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/55/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%88%D9%84-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A3%D8%B1%D9%83%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D9%83%D9%85","ar":"أما نفس الحكم فقد ذكرناه وأنه يرجع إلى الخطاب","en":"As for the ruling itself, we have already explained that it is reducible to an address.","terms":["hukm","khitab","taklif"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"What “address” means","text":"Khaṭāb should not automatically be glossed as an enacted statute or a subjective quality of the act. It refers here to the Lawgiver’s communicative address within al-Ghazālī’s theory of obligation."},{"type":"critical","label":"Boundary of the extract","text":"The governing discussion occupies a larger unit than this opening phrase; the present reader does not claim that the whole account has been translated."}],"group":"Selected loci","coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"E01","part":"evidence","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Consensus as an object of proof","headingAr":"القطب الثاني في الأدلة","sourcePage":"I:148","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/127/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AF%D9%84%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A5%D8%AC%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%B9-%D9%88%D9%81%D9%8A%D9%87-%D8%A3%D8%A8%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%A8","ar":"ومن حاول إثبات كون الإجماع حجة افتقر إلى تفهيم لفظ الإجماع أولا وبيان تصوره ثانيا","en":"Whoever seeks to establish consensus as an authoritative proof must first explain the term and what is conceived by it.","terms":["ijma","hujjiyya","hadd"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Concept first, demonstration next","text":"He explicitly distinguishes conceptual determination from the separate attempt to prove its authority. This reprises the logical introduction."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"E02","part":"evidence","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"The meaning of consensus","headingAr":"القطب الثاني في الأدلة","sourcePage":"I:148","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/127/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AF%D9%84%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A5%D8%AC%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%B9-%D9%88%D9%81%D9%8A%D9%87-%D8%A3%D8%A8%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%A8","ar":"فإنما نعني به اتفاق أمة محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم خاصة على أمر من الأمور الدينية","en":"By consensus here we mean specifically the agreement of Muḥammad’s community upon a matter of religion.","terms":["ijma"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"A formula requiring subsequent qualification","text":"The later discussion asks who counts in constituting the consensus and what form of agreement must be made evident."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"E03","part":"evidence","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Silence is not spoken agreement","headingAr":"القطب الثاني في الأدلة","sourcePage":"I:156","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/139/%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%A3%D9%84%D8%A9-%D9%84%D8%A7-%D8%AD%D8%AC%D8%A9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A5%D8%AC%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%B9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A8%D8%B9%D8%AF-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B5%D8%AD%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%A9","ar":"وتمام النظر في هذا الركن ببيان أن السكوت ليس كالنطق وأن انقراض العصر ليس بشرط","en":"A full treatment of this constituent requires showing that silence is not the equivalent of an expressed opinion, and that the passing away of an entire generation is not a condition.","terms":["ijma","hujjiyya"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Consensus requires positive evidence","text":"Not every failure to object constitutes a binding agreement. He separately analyses testimony, timing, and mode of expression."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"E04","part":"evidence","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Three positions of legal interest relative to the sources","headingAr":"القطب الثاني في الأدلة","sourcePage":"I:173–174","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/159/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B5%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD","ar":"القسم الثالث: ما لم يشهد له من الشرع بالبطلان ولا بالاعتبار نص معين وهذا في محل النظر","en":"The third class comprises considerations for which no particular revealed text attests either rejection or acceptance. This is the very point that requires investigation.","terms":["maslaha","maqasid"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"The disputed case is intermediate","text":"Neither the existence of apparent benefit nor its absence from a particular text settles the question of probative legitimacy."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"E05","part":"evidence","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Why an expedient preference cannot override text","headingAr":"القطب الثاني في الأدلة","sourcePage":"I:174","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/159/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B5%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD","ar":"فكانت المصلحة في إيجاب الصوم لينزجر به، فهذا قول باطل ومخالف لنص الكتاب بالمصلحة","en":"It was claimed that requiring fasting would restrain the offender more effectively; but that judgment is unsound when it sets an alleged interest against the express text.","terms":["maslaha","hujjiyya"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Example rather than endorsement","text":"The quoted argument is a counterexample rejected by al-Ghazālī; the new translation distinguishes interlocutor claims from the author’s conclusion."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"E06","part":"evidence","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Why maṣlaḥa cannot be an autonomous fifth source","headingAr":"القطب الثاني في الأدلة","sourcePage":"I:175–176","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/159/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B5%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD","ar":"إذ من ظن أنه أصل خامس فقد أخطأ؛ لأنا رددنا المصلحة إلى حفظ مقاصد الشرع","en":"To suppose that interest constitutes a fifth foundational source is an error, since we have referred legally relevant interest back to preserving the purposes of the revealed Law.","terms":["maslaha","maqasid","hujjiyya"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Independence versus relevance","text":"The point is about independent evidentiary authority, not a claim that preservation of the law’s purposes is unimportant."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"E07","part":"evidence","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Where the law’s purposes are known","headingAr":"القطب الثاني في الأدلة","sourcePage":"I:175–176","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/159/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B5%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD","ar":"ومقاصد الشرع تعرف بالكتاب والسنة والإجماع","en":"The purposes of the revealed Law are known through the Book, the Sunna, and consensus.","terms":["maqasid","ijma"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Against detached teleology","text":"An assertion of an attractive end does not automatically establish that end as an authorized legal purpose."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"M06","part":"maslaha","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"Ordinary advantage is not the technical criterion","headingAr":"الاستصلاح","sourcePage":"I:174","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/159/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B5%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD","ar":"أما المصلحة فهي عبارة في الأصل عن جلب منفعة أو دفع مضرة، ولسنا نعني به ذلك، فإن جلب المنفعة ودفع المضرة مقاصد الخلق وصلاح الخلق في تحصيل مقاصدهم، لكنا نعني بالمصلحة المحافظة على مقصود الشرع.","en":"In its original sense, maṣlaḥa is bringing about an advantage or warding off a harm. That, however, is not what we intend here. Acquiring advantage and avoiding harm are the aims of human beings, and their well-being consists in attaining the ends they seek. By maṣlaḥa we instead mean preserving what the revealed Law intends.","terms":["maslaha","maqasid","masad"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Normative source of the end","text":"This is not a simple identification of benefits with subjective preference. He specifies a juridically authoritative criterion related to the Lawgiver’s ends."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"M07","part":"maslaha","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"The five protected fundamentals","headingAr":"الاستصلاح","sourcePage":"I:174","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/159/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B5%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD","ar":"ومقصود الشرع من الخلق خمسة: وهو أن يحفظ عليهم دينهم ونفسهم وعقلهم ونسلهم ومالهم، فكل ما يتضمن حفظ هذه الأصول الخمسة فهو مصلحة، وكل ما يفوت هذه الأصول فهو مفسدة ودفعها مصلحة.","en":"The purposes of the Law concerning human beings are five: to preserve for them religion, life, intellect, lineage, and property. Whatever secures these five fundamentals is a legal interest (maṣlaḥa); whatever undermines them is a corruption or harm (mafsada), and preventing that harm is itself a legal interest.","terms":["maqasid","maslaha","masad"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Five purposes are not five freestanding sources","text":"This is a criterion within the demonstration of admissible interests; in the later passage Ghazālī resists constructing an independent fifth source from unrestricted assessments of benefit."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"M01","part":"maslaha","group":"Interests and legal ends","title":"Common benefit and technical maṣlaḥa","headingAr":"الأصل الرابع من الأصول الموهومة: الاستصلاح","sourcePage":"I:174","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.com/ar/library/content/33/159/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B5%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD","ar":"أما المصلحة فهي عبارة في الأصل عن جلب منفعة أو دفع مضرة، ولسنا نعني به ذلك، فإن جلب المنفعة ودفع المضرة مقاصد الخلق وصلاح الخلق في تحصيل مقاصدهم.","en":"In its primary usage, maṣlaḥa means securing a benefit or averting a harm. But this is not what we intend by it here. For securing benefits and averting harms are people’s own aims, and their welfare consists in attaining what they seek.","terms":["maslaha","maqasid"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Two distinct senses","text":"Al-Ghazālī explicitly sets aside the common human-benefit meaning before defining the narrower legal-technical sense. The contrast is fundamental to any comparison with later utilitarian or Thomist accounts of the common good."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"M02","part":"maslaha","group":"Interests and legal ends","title":"The five protected fundamentals","headingAr":"الأصل الرابع من الأصول الموهومة: الاستصلاح","sourcePage":"I:174","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.com/ar/library/content/33/159/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B5%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD","ar":"لكنا نعني بالمصلحة المحافظة على مقصود الشرع، ومقصود الشرع من الخلق خمسة: وهو أن يحفظ عليهم دينهم ونفسهم وعقلهم ونسلهم ومالهم. فكل ما يتضمن حفظ هذه الأصول الخمسة فهو مصلحة، وكل ما يفوت هذه الأصول فهو مفسدة ودفعها مصلحة.","en":"What we mean by maṣlaḥa, however, is safeguarding the purpose of the revealed Law. The Law’s purposes for human beings are five: that their religion, life, intellect, progeny, and property be safeguarded. Whatever protects these five fundamentals is a maṣlaḥa; whatever undermines them is a mafsada, a harm or corruption; and averting such corruption is itself a maṣlaḥa.","terms":["maslaha","maqasid","illah"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Purpose is not necessarily operative legal cause","text":"Neither the five ends nor the mere presence of benefit automatically gives a particular act a valid ʿilla. Al-Ghazālī’s argument about the relevance and ranking of interests continues beyond this excerpt."},{"type":"crosswork","label":"Shifāʾ al-Ghalīl: directly comparable passage","text":"In Shifāʾ al-Ghalīl, he asks what makes a meaning legally suitable and speaks of meanings pointing toward the aspects and signs of interests. This comparison is grounded in a distinct primary passage, not presented as a quotation from al-Mustaṣfā."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"M03","part":"maslaha","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"The three ranks of interests","headingAr":"الاستصلاح","sourcePage":"I:174","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/159/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B5%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD","ar":"المصلحة باعتبار قوتها في ذاتها تنقسم إلى ما هي في رتبة الضرورات وإلى ما هي في رتبة الحاجات وإلى ما يتعلق بالتحسينات والتزيينات","en":"Considered according to their intrinsic weight, interests are divided into necessities, needs, and those pertaining to refinement and adornment.","terms":["maslaha","maqasid"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Ranks, not social classes","text":"Darūrāt / ḥājāt / taḥsīnāt is a grading of legal interests or ends, not a typology of persons or castes."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"M04","part":"maslaha","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"The five fundamentals and deprivation","headingAr":"الاستصلاح","sourcePage":"I:174–175","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/159/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B5%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD","ar":"فكل ما يتضمن حفظ هذه الأصول الخمسة فهو مصلحة وكل ما يفوت هذه الأصول فهو مفسدة ودفعها مصلحة","en":"Whatever sustains these five foundational objects is an interest; whatever defeats them is a corruption, and preventing that corruption is also an interest.","terms":["maslaha","maqasid"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Maintenance and prevention","text":"Notice the two directions of legal concern: maintaining the fundamental good and preventing an injury to it."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"M05","part":"maslaha","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Conflicts require prioritization","headingAr":"الاستصلاح","sourcePage":"I:175–176","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/159/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B5%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD","ar":"وحيث ذكرنا خلافا فذلك عند تعارض مصلحتين ومقصودين، وعند ذلك ترجيح الأقوى","en":"Where we have spoken of disagreement, it concerns conflict between two interests or two purposes; in that event, the stronger claim must receive priority.","terms":["maslaha","maqasid"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Specified context","text":"Strength is adjudicated within the system of law’s recognized ends and proofs, not by an unregulated modern cost-benefit calculation."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"M08","part":"maslaha","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"Why maṣlaḥa is not an independent fifth source","headingAr":"الاستصلاح","sourcePage":"I:179","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/159/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B5%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD","ar":"إذ من ظن أنه أصل خامس فقد أخطأ؛ لأنا رددنا المصلحة إلى حفظ مقاصد الشرع، ومقاصد الشرع تعرف بالكتاب والسنة والإجماع. فكل مصلحة لا ترجع إلى حفظ مقصود فهم من الكتاب والسنة والإجماع وكانت من المصالح الغريبة التي لا تلائم تصرفات الشرع فهي باطلة مطرحة.","en":"Anyone who supposes it to be a fifth independent source is mistaken. We have reduced maṣlaḥa to the preservation of the Law’s purposes; and those purposes are known from the Book, the Sunna, and consensus. Consequently, an alleged interest that cannot be referred back to a purpose understood from those sources, but instead stands alien to the Law’s established manner of acting, is rejected as invalid.","terms":["maslaha","maqasid","istislah","ijma"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Limits to creative legal policy","text":"The status of an unenumerated interest must be proved through its affinity with juridically established ends; the term does not license free-standing judicial legislation."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"Q05","part":"inference","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"The operative definition of juristic analogy","headingAr":"القطب الثالث في الدلالات","sourcePage":"I:281","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/252/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D9%83%D9%8A%D9%81%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%AB%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AD%D9%83%D8%A7%D9%85-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%84%D9%81%D8%A7%D8%B8-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%82%D8%AA%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B3-%D9%85%D9%86-%D9%85%D8%B9%D9%82%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%84%D9%81%D8%A7%D8%B8-%D8%A8%D8%B7%D8%B1%D9%8A%D9%82-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D9%8A%D8%A7%D8%B3","ar":"وحده أنه حمل معلوم على معلوم في إثبات حكم لهما أو نفيه عنهما بأمر جامع بينهما من إثبات حكم أو صفة أو نفيهما عنهما.","en":"Its definition is to carry what is known of one case over to another known case, in affirming or negating a ruling in respect of the two, by virtue of a feature that joins them, whether that commonality is the affirmation or negation of a ruling or of a characteristic.","terms":["qiyas","illah","hukm"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Legal analogising versus formal syllogism","text":"This is a definition of relating cases through a shared factor. Compare the quite different definition of a syllogism in Miḥakk; the same Arabic word can name different intellectual operations."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"Q06","part":"inference","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"Not every association warrants analogical transfer","headingAr":"القطب الثالث في الدلالات","sourcePage":"I:281","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/252/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D9%83%D9%8A%D9%81%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%AB%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AD%D9%83%D8%A7%D9%85-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%84%D9%81%D8%A7%D8%B8-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%82%D8%AA%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B3-%D9%85%D9%86-%D9%85%D8%B9%D9%82%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%84%D9%81%D8%A7%D8%B8-%D8%A8%D8%B7%D8%B1%D9%8A%D9%82-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D9%8A%D8%A7%D8%B3","ar":"ثم إن كان الجامع موجبا للاجتماع على الحكم كان قياسا صحيحا وإلا كان فاسدا، واسم القياس يشتمل على الصحيح والفاسد في اللغة.","en":"If the shared element actually warrants the cases being joined in their ruling, the analogy is sound; otherwise it is defective. In ordinary usage, the word “analogy” can apply to both sound and unsound forms.","terms":["qiyas","sihha","fasad"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Soundness demands a relevant common factor","text":"Being able to name a feature common to two cases is not sufficient; the relation to the legal ruling must be warranted rather than merely imagined."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"Q07","part":"inference","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"The four indispensable members of an analogy","headingAr":"القطب الثالث في الدلالات","sourcePage":"I:281","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/252/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D9%83%D9%8A%D9%81%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%AB%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%B1-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AD%D9%83%D8%A7%D9%85-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%84%D9%81%D8%A7%D8%B8-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%82%D8%AA%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B3-%D9%85%D9%86-%D9%85%D8%B9%D9%82%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%84%D9%81%D8%A7%D8%B8-%D8%A8%D8%B7%D8%B1%D9%8A%D9%82-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D9%8A%D8%A7%D8%B3","ar":"ولا بد في كل قياس من فرع وأصل وعلة وحكم، وليس من شرط الفرع والأصل كونهما موجودين بل ربما يستدل بالنفي على النفي.","en":"Every analogy requires a new case (farʿ), an original case (aṣl), a legal ground (ʿilla), and a ruling (ḥukm). It is not a condition of the new case or the original that the object actually exists: one may sometimes infer one negation from another.","terms":["qiyas","illah","hukm","asl","far"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Do not infer metaphysical causality from the word ʿilla","text":"The legal ground is a function in a juridical inference. It need not signify an efficient cause in natural philosophy or the full wisdom behind legislation."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"Q01","part":"inference","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Four grades of analogical reasoning","headingAr":"القطب الثالث في الدلالات","sourcePage":"I:320","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/280/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B7%D8%B1%D9%81-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%8A-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AA%D8%AF%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%AC-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D9%85%D9%86%D8%A7%D8%B2%D9%84-%D9%87%D8%B0%D9%87-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%82%D9%8A%D8%B3%D8%A9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A3%D8%B9%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%87%D8%A7-%D8%A5%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A3%D8%AF%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%87%D8%A7","ar":"وبيانه أن القياس أربعة أنواع: المؤثر ثم المناسب ثم الشبه ثم الطرد","en":"The explanation is that analogical inference has four kinds: the demonstrably effective, the suitably related, the resembling, and bare concomitance.","terms":["qiyas","illah","zann"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"A graded series","text":"Do not make the four kinds equally probative. Al-Ghazālī orders them by how securely the attribute is tied to rulings recognized by law."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"Q02","part":"inference","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"The higher end of the spectrum","headingAr":"القطب الثالث في الدلالات","sourcePage":"I:320","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/280/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B7%D8%B1%D9%81-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%8A-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AA%D8%AF%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%AC-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D9%85%D9%86%D8%A7%D8%B2%D9%84-%D9%87%D8%B0%D9%87-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%82%D9%8A%D8%B3%D8%A9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A3%D8%B9%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%87%D8%A7-%D8%A5%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A3%D8%AF%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%87%D8%A7","ar":"وأدناها الطرد الذي ينبغي أن ينكره كل قائل بالقياس وأعلاها ما في معنى الأصل","en":"At the weakest extreme lies mere regular concomitance, which even proponents of analogy ought to reject; at the strongest stands a case included in the meaning of the original.","terms":["qiyas","illah"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Critique from within the method","text":"Al-Ghazālī argues against mechanically extending the law on the basis of attributes that lack a plausible evidentiary connection to the rule."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"Q03","part":"inference","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Recognizing effective legal attributes","headingAr":"القطب الثالث في الدلالات","sourcePage":"I:320","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/280/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B7%D8%B1%D9%81-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%8A-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AA%D8%AF%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%AC-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D9%85%D9%86%D8%A7%D8%B2%D9%84-%D9%87%D8%B0%D9%87-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%82%D9%8A%D8%B3%D8%A9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A3%D8%B9%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%87%D8%A7-%D8%A5%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A3%D8%AF%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%87%D8%A7","ar":"والمؤثر يعرف كونه مؤثرا بنص أو إجماع أو سبر حاصر","en":"An attribute is established as legally effective through an explicit text, consensus, or an exhaustive process of elimination among the possible candidates.","terms":["qiyas","illah","ijma"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Sabr ḥāṣir","text":"Here it denotes exhaustive consideration and elimination, not ordinary observation or statistical survey alone."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"Q04","part":"inference","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Analogy generates different degrees of probability","headingAr":"القطب الثالث في الدلالات","sourcePage":"I:321","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/280/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B7%D8%B1%D9%81-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%8A-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AA%D8%AF%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%AC-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D9%85%D9%86%D8%A7%D8%B2%D9%84-%D9%87%D8%B0%D9%87-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%82%D9%8A%D8%B3%D8%A9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A3%D8%B9%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%87%D8%A7-%D8%A5%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A3%D8%AF%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%87%D8%A7","ar":"فلأجل ذلك تتفاوت درجات الظن والأعلى مقدم على الأسفل والأقرب مقدم على الأبعد في الجنسية","en":"Accordingly, probable judgments admit different degrees: the stronger takes precedence over the weaker, and what belongs to a more closely related kind over what belongs to a more remote one.","terms":["zann","qiyas","illah"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Method of legal inquiry","text":"This is a central bridge from logical gradation to juristic choice, without pretending that such premises yield apodictic necessity."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"I05","part":"ijtihad","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"Three arts of the final division","headingAr":"القطب الرابع في المجتهد","sourcePage":"I:342","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/299/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%AD%D9%83%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%AB%D9%85%D8%B1-%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%AC%D8%AA%D9%87%D8%AF","ar":"ويشتمل هذا القطب على ثلاثة فنون: فن في الاجتهاد، وفن في التقليد، وفن في ترجيح المجتهد دليلا على دليل عند التعارض.","en":"This principal division comprises three arts: one investigating juristic effort (ijtihād), another following an authority (taqlīd), and a third the jurist’s preference of one proof over another when they conflict.","terms":["ijtihad","muqallid","mujtahid","tarjih"],"notes":[{"type":"context","label":"The roles of inquiry, reception, and conflict resolution","text":"Al-Ghazālī does not equate every legally relevant act of knowledge with ijtihād. A lay follower and a qualified inquirer have different relations to the proof."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"I06","part":"ijtihad","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"Exertion must involve real difficulty","headingAr":"القطب الرابع في المجتهد","sourcePage":"I:342","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/299/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%AD%D9%83%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%AB%D9%85%D8%B1-%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%AC%D8%AA%D9%87%D8%AF","ar":"وهو عبارة عن بذل المجهود واستفراغ الوسع في فعل من الأفعال، ولا يستعمل إلا فيما فيه كلفة وجهد، فيقال: اجتهد في حمل حجر الرحا، ولا يقال: اجتهد في حمل خردلة.","en":"In its ordinary sense, ijtihād is to expend one’s utmost effort and exhaust one’s capacity in an action. The word is used only where the task entails genuine difficulty and exertion: one says that someone strove to lift a millstone, but not that he strove to lift a mustard seed.","terms":["ijtihad"],"notes":[{"type":"translation","label":"Ordinary sense before juristic specification","text":"The millstone/mustard-seed contrast explains the verbal sense of exertion, not an independent legal criterion of great bodily hardship."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"I07","part":"ijtihad","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"The full technical definition of juristic effort","headingAr":"القطب الرابع في المجتهد","sourcePage":"I:342","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/299/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%AD%D9%83%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%AB%D9%85%D8%B1-%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%AC%D8%AA%D9%87%D8%AF","ar":"لكن صار اللفظ في عرف العلماء مخصوصا ببذل المجتهد وسعه في طلب العلم بأحكام الشريعة، والاجتهاد التام أن يبذل الوسع في الطلب بحيث يحس من نفسه بالعجز عن مزيد طلب.","en":"In the scholars’ technical usage, the expression has become specific to the qualified jurist’s expenditure of his capacity in seeking knowledge of the Law’s rulings. Fully accomplished ijtihād consists in pursuing the inquiry to the point where the inquirer recognizes that no further search lies within his capacity.","terms":["ijtihad","mujtahid","hukm"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Epistemic diligence is an activity, not guaranteed truth","text":"Completion describes exhaustive inquiry by a qualified subject, not a claim that every conclusion thereby becomes a necessary demonstration."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"I08","part":"ijtihad","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"The first qualification: mastery of legal evidence","headingAr":"القطب الرابع في المجتهد","sourcePage":"I:343","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/300/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%86%D8%B8%D8%B1-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%88%D9%84-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A3%D8%B1%D9%83%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AC%D8%AA%D9%87%D8%A7%D8%AF-%D9%88%D8%A3%D8%AD%D9%83%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%87","ar":"أحدهما أن يكون محيطا بمدارك الشرع متمكنا من استثارة الظن بالنظر فيها وتقديم ما يجب تقديمه وتأخير ما يجب تأخيره.","en":"The first condition is that the jurist encompass the avenues by which the Law is known, possess the capacity to elicit a preponderant conclusion by examining them, and know how to give priority to what deserves priority and defer what should come later.","terms":["mujtahid","zann","hujjiyya"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"A craft of graded discernment","text":"The ability to elicit ẓann by ordered consideration of proofs is a positive qualification. It does not equate conjecture with arbitrary opinion."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"I09","part":"ijtihad","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"The second qualification: whose fatwā may be relied upon","headingAr":"القطب الرابع في المجتهد","sourcePage":"I:343","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/300/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%86%D8%B8%D8%B1-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%88%D9%84-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A3%D8%B1%D9%83%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AC%D8%AA%D9%87%D8%A7%D8%AF-%D9%88%D8%A3%D8%AD%D9%83%D8%A7%D9%85%D9%87","ar":"الثاني: أن يكون عدلا مجتنبا للمعاصي القادحة في العدالة، وهذا يشترط لجواز الاعتماد على فتواه فمن ليس عدلا فلا تقبل فتواه، أما هو في نفسه فلا، فكأن العدالة شرط القبول للفتوى لا شرط صحة الاجتهاد.","en":"The second is that the jurist be upright, avoiding sins that compromise probity. This is required for others to be entitled to rely upon his fatwā: the fatwā of someone without such uprightness is not accepted. That restriction does not apply to the jurist’s reasoning as it concerns him personally. Uprightness is thus a condition of the fatwā’s acceptance, rather than a condition of the validity of the act of ijtihād.","terms":["mujtahid","ijtihad","adala","fatwa"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Two distinct kinds of condition","text":"A requirement governing public reliance on a fatwā must not be silently transformed into a defect of every intellectual operation of a morally compromised scholar."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"I10","part":"ijtihad","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"The proper subject of juristic effort","headingAr":"القطب الرابع في المجتهد","sourcePage":"I:344","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/301/%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%A3%D9%84%D8%A9-%D8%AC%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%B2-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AA%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF-%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D9%8A%D8%A7%D8%B3-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AC%D8%AA%D9%87%D8%A7%D8%AF-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%B2%D9%85%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%B3%D9%88%D9%84","ar":"والمجتهد فيه كل حكم شرعي ليس فيه دليل قطعي، واحترزنا بالشرعي عن العقليات ومسائل الكلام، فإن الحق فيها واحد والمصيب واحد والمخطئ آثم.","en":"The proper subject of ijtihād is every legal ruling for which no decisive proof is available. The qualification “legal” excludes questions of rational demonstration and theology: in those domains, according to this argument, the truth is one, and there is one who has it right, while the person in error is blameworthy.","terms":["ijtihad","qat_i","hukm","kalam"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Delimiting rather than universalizing fallibilism","text":"The claim concerns an epistemic/legal domain. It must be read alongside the following distinction concerning obvious obligations established by decisive proofs."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"I03","part":"ijtihad","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Subject matter of juristic inquiry","headingAr":"القطب الرابع في المجتهد","sourcePage":"I:346","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/301/","ar":"والمجتهد فيه كل حكم شرعي ليس فيه دليل قطعي","en":"A question open to qualified juristic inquiry is any legal ruling for which no decisive proof is available.","terms":["ijtihad","yaqin"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"A limit on ijtihād","text":"The legal question remains open to inquiry because its evidence is not demonstratively conclusive, not merely because jurists happen to disagree."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"I04","part":"ijtihad","group":"Source-anchored selection","title":"Reasoned error and conclusive proof","headingAr":"القطب الرابع في المجتهد","sourcePage":"I:346","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/301/","ar":"ووجوب الصلوات الخمس والزكوات وما اتفقت عليه الأمة من جليات الشرع فيها أدلة قطعية","en":"The obligation of the five prayers, the alms dues, and the manifest principles agreed upon by the community are established by decisive proofs.","terms":["ijtihad","yaqin","ijma"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Distinguishing the established from the disputable","text":"This statement limits the sphere in which disagreement can be excused as an exercise of ijtihād."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"Working online Arabic transcription excerpt; targeted source checks only, not independently collated against a printed witness","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"I11","part":"ijtihad","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"Complete inquiry in its place removes blame","headingAr":"القطب الرابع في المجتهد","sourcePage":"I:348","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/303/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D9%83%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%88%D9%84-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%AA%D8%A3%D8%AB%D9%8A%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%AE%D8%B7%D8%A6-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AC%D8%AA%D9%87%D8%A7%D8%AF","ar":"والإثم ينتفي عن كل من جمع صفات المجتهدين إذا تمم الاجتهاد في محله، فكل اجتهاد تام إذا صدر من أهله وصادف محله فثمرته حق وصواب، والإثم عن المجتهد منفي.","en":"Blame is removed from everyone who possesses the qualifications of a mujtahid when he completes his inquiry in the matters to which ijtihād properly applies. Every complete instance of ijtihād undertaken by a competent jurist within its legitimate domain yields an acceptable fruit and a right result, and the jurist incurs no sin for it.","terms":["ijtihad","mujtahid","zann","taswib"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Do not detach taṣwīb from its conditions","text":"Al-Ghazālī requires competent agent, complete effort and proper subject matter. The sentence cannot be cited as an unrestricted endorsement of all conflicting opinions."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"I01","part":"ijtihad","group":"Probable judgment and legal inference","title":"An inquiry into predominant probability","headingAr":"مسألة نفي حكم معين في المجتهدات","sourcePage":"I:362–363","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/309/%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%A3%D9%84%D8%A9-%D9%86%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%AD%D9%83%D9%85-%D9%85%D8%B9%D9%8A%D9%86-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%AC%D8%AA%D9%87%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%AA","ar":"قلنا: فقد أخطأتم إذ ظننتم أن المجتهد يطلب حكم الله مع علمه بأن حكم الله خطابه، فإن الواقعة لا نص فيها ولا خطاب بل إنما يطلب غلبة الظن.","en":"We reply: You have erred in supposing that the mujtahid seeks God’s ruling while knowing that God’s ruling is His address. In the case under discussion there is no determinate text and no particular address. What the jurist seeks, rather, is a preponderance of probability.","terms":["ijtihad","zann","hukm","khitab"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Bounded to the disputed case","text":"This is a passage in the debate on legal correctness and error when no determinate textual ruling is posited. It is not a universal claim that revelation provides no binding rulings."},{"type":"scope","label":"Qualified thesis in a juristic dispute","text":"This discussion treats the thesis of taṣwīb (and its opponents) in cases lacking a settled revelatory ruling. It is not a declaration that every legal rule or disagreement is subjectively constituted."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"I02","part":"ijtihad","group":"Probable judgment and legal inference","title":"The sea-crossing analogy","headingAr":"مسألة نفي حكم معين في المجتهدات","sourcePage":"I:362–363","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/309/%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%A3%D9%84%D8%A9-%D9%86%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%AD%D9%83%D9%85-%D9%85%D8%B9%D9%8A%D9%86-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%AC%D8%AA%D9%87%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%AA","ar":"وهو كمن كان على ساحل البحر وقيل له: إن غلب على ظنك السلامة أبيح لك الركوب، وإن غلب على ظنك الهلاك حرم عليك الركوب. وقبل حصول الظن لا حكم لله عليك وإنما حكمه يترتب على ظنك ويتبع ظنك بعد حصوله، فهو يطلب الظن دون الإباحة والتحريم.","en":"He is like someone standing at the shore who is told: “If you predominantly judge the crossing to be safe, you may embark; but if you predominantly judge that it will destroy you, embarking is forbidden.” Before such a judgment is formed, no ruling applies to you on that basis; rather, its application follows the probable judgment once it has arisen. The inquiry thus seeks the judgment of probability, not permission or prohibition themselves.","terms":["zann","ijtihad","hukm"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Norm, evidence and condition","text":"The illustration should not be mistaken for legal voluntarism. Its conditional norm is specified, while the jurist’s judgment establishes whether its condition is satisfied."}],"coverage":"selected excerpt","verification":"initial independently rendered working extract; Arabic source linked, no manuscript collation","hammadPrintedPage":null},{"id":"I12","part":"ijtihad","group":"Source-checked argumentative paragraph","title":"Why adjudicated decisions may stand","headingAr":"القطب الرابع في المجتهد","sourcePage":"I:368","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/311/%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%A3%D9%84%D8%A9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D9%86%D9%82%D8%B6-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AC%D8%AA%D9%87%D8%A7%D8%AF","ar":"ولو حكم بصحة النكاح حاكم بعد أن خالع الزوج ثلاثا ثم تغير اجتهاده لم يفرق بين الزوجين ولم ينقض اجتهاده السابق بصحة النكاح لمصلحة الحكم، فإنه لو نقض الاجتهاد لنقض النقض أيضا ولتسلسل فاضطربت الأحكام ولم يوثق بها.","en":"If a judge had already ruled the marriage valid following a triple separation by khulʿ, and subsequently changed his juristic assessment, the couple would not be separated and his earlier decision would not be annulled. This protects the public good of authoritative judgment: if an earlier act of ijtihād could simply be overturned by a later one, the overturning could itself be overturned, producing an endless sequence in which legal decisions become unstable and lose people’s confidence.","terms":["ijtihad","hukm","maslaha","fatwa"],"notes":[{"type":"critical","label":"Institutional closure and revisability of reasoning","text":"The jurist may change his view without retroactively depriving a completed judicial decision of finality. It is a legal-institutional principle, not a guarantee that the earlier opinion was demonstratively true."}],"coverage":"selected paragraph","verification":"Directly checked in online Arabic transcription at linked location; no printed-witness collation","hammadPrintedPage":null}],"terms":[{"id":"usul","ar":"أصول الفقه","tr":"uṣūl al-fiqh","en":"principles of jurisprudence","quick":"The systematic study of the legal rulings’ proofs, their modes of indication, and conditions of qualified legal inference.","worklinks":[["mankhul","earlier treatment","Bibliographic"],["ihya","different disciplinary framing","Contextual"]]},{"id":"qutb","ar":"قطب","tr":"quṭb","en":"principal division / axis","quick":"One of the four coordinated main divisions in al-Mustaṣfā’s architecture.","worklinks":[]},{"id":"kalam","ar":"كلام","tr":"kalām","en":"dialectical theology","quick":"The discipline that, in this classification, investigates matters most general within religious sciences.","worklinks":[["iqtisad","theological exposition","Topical"],["ihya","evaluations of kalām","Contextual"]]},{"id":"kulli","ar":"كلي","tr":"kullī","en":"universal","quick":"Treating the most general scope of a field, contrasted here with the more circumscribed objects of particular sciences.","worklinks":[["ihya","differing disciplinary evaluations","Contextual"]]},{"id":"hukm","ar":"حكم","tr":"ḥukm","en":"legal ruling","quick":"Here, the normative ruling considered in relation to the divine address, as distinguished from an act’s natural property.","worklinks":[["mankhul","early legal theory","Topical"],["iqtisad","lawgiver and divine speech","Contextual"]]},{"id":"khitab","ar":"خطاب","tr":"khiṭāb","en":"address","quick":"The Lawgiver’s normative address; not automatically a modern legislative statute.","worklinks":[["iqtisad","divine speech and obligation","Contextual"]]},{"id":"taklif","ar":"تكليف","tr":"taklīf","en":"moral-legal obligation","quick":"Binding address to accountable persons; its preconditions are discussed within the legal ruling.","worklinks":[["iqtisad","theological conditions","Topical"],["ihya","practical obligation","Contextual"]]},{"id":"ijma","ar":"إجماع","tr":"ijmāʿ","en":"consensus","quick":"A source claimed to carry normative evidentiary authority; its ḥujjiyya requires argument.","worklinks":[["mankhul","early legal theory","Topical"]]},{"id":"hujjiyya","ar":"حجية","tr":"ḥujjiyya","en":"probative authority","quick":"The standing of something as an authoritative ground for argument and legal determination.","worklinks":[["mankhul","earlier treatment","Topical"]]},{"id":"qiyas","ar":"قياس","tr":"qiyās","en":"analogy / syllogistic inference","quick":"Different domains use qiyās differently: formal syllogism in logic, analogical extension in juristic reasoning.","worklinks":[["asas","extended analysis of legal analogy","Topical"],["shifa","suitability and causal reason","Topical"],["miyar","logical inference","Topical"]]},{"id":"hadd","ar":"حد","tr":"ḥadd","en":"definition","quick":"An account identifying what a thing is; treated as a prerequisite for precise theoretical discourse.","worklinks":[["miyar","named earlier logical work","Explicit"],["mihakk","named earlier logical work","Explicit"]]},{"id":"burhan","ar":"برهان","tr":"burhān","en":"demonstration","quick":"An inference formed from premises in a valid configuration; note the distinct certainty or probability of its premises.","worklinks":[["miyar","named earlier logical work","Explicit"],["mihakk","named earlier logical work","Explicit"],["qistas","alternative presentation of inference","Topical"]]},{"id":"yaqin","ar":"يقين","tr":"yaqīn","en":"certainty","quick":"Certain cognition; not to be indiscriminately transferred to all conclusions of juristic ẓann.","worklinks":[["miyar","degrees of assent","Topical"],["munqidh","knowledge and certainty","Contextual"]]},{"id":"zann","ar":"ظن","tr":"ẓann","en":"probable judgment","quick":"Defeasible but reasoned assent that may nevertheless furnish an operative juristic basis.","worklinks":[["miyar","types and strengths of assent","Topical"],["qistas","forms of inference","Contextual"]]},{"id":"maslaha","ar":"مصلحة","tr":"maṣlaḥa","en":"legally cognizable interest","quick":"For al-Ghazālī here: preservation of the Lawgiver’s purposes, not simple maximization of subjective utility.","worklinks":[["shifa","legal suitability and interests","Topical"],["asas","relation to analogy","Topical"],["mizan","ethics of human goods","Contextual"]]},{"id":"maqasid","ar":"مقاصد","tr":"maqāṣid","en":"purposes / ends","quick":"The purposes of the Lawgiver, here concretely articulated by five protected necessities.","worklinks":[["shifa","recognition of legally relevant interests","Topical"],["ihya","human formation and practical ends","Contextual"]]},{"id":"illah","ar":"علة","tr":"ʿilla","en":"operative reason","quick":"A legal reason or effective basis, not necessarily identical with a broader wisdom or purpose.","worklinks":[["shifa","causal reasoning in analogy","Topical"],["asas","legal analogy","Topical"]]},{"id":"ijtihad","ar":"اجتهاد","tr":"ijtihād","en":"qualified juristic inquiry","quick":"Exertion of trained juridical capacity in determining rulings under relevant sources and evidence.","worklinks":[["mankhul","early discussions of legal inquiry","Topical"],["shifa","operational analogy","Contextual"]]},{"id":"fiqh","ar":"فقه","tr":"fiqh","en":"jurisprudence; understanding","quick":"Lexically knowing and understanding; technically knowledge of rulings attaching to the acts of accountable persons.","worklinks":[["mankhul","jurisprudence as discipline","Topical"],["ihya","religious significance of learning","Contextual"]]},{"id":"dalala","ar":"دلالة","tr":"dalāla","en":"indication / signification","quick":"The relation by which proof or wording signifies a ruling. Its modes are a core part of the third axis.","worklinks":[["mankhul","linguistic legal indications","Topical"],["miyar","logico-semantic contrasts","Contextual"]]},{"id":"tasawwur","ar":"تصور","tr":"taṣawwur","en":"conception","quick":"The grasp of a single content or meaning without asserting it to be true or false.","worklinks":[["mihakk","conceiving simple terms","Verified verbal parallel"],["miyar","division of cognition","Topical"]]},{"id":"tasdiq","ar":"تصديق","tr":"taṣdīq","en":"assent / propositional judgment","quick":"Taking an affirmative or negative proposition to be true; epistemic degrees of assent require further distinctions.","worklinks":[["mihakk","assent and predication","Verified verbal parallel"],["mankhul","knowledge and conviction","Topical"]]},{"id":"awwaliyat","ar":"أوليات","tr":"awwaliyyāt","en":"first principles","quick":"Judgments known without argumentative mediation, contrasted with truths established through investigation.","worklinks":[["mihakk","materials of demonstration","Verified verbal parallel"]]},{"id":"mawadd","ar":"مقدمات","tr":"muqaddimāt","en":"premises","quick":"The judgments from which an inference is composed; their strength as premises determines the status of its conclusion.","worklinks":[["mihakk","syllogistic matter and form","Verified verbal parallel"]]},{"id":"mujtahid","ar":"مجتهد","tr":"mujtahid","en":"qualified juristic inquirer","quick":"A jurist possessing the pertinent interpretative and evidentiary competences to form responsible legal judgments.","worklinks":[["mankhul","qualification for independent inquiry","Topical"]]},{"id":"muqallid","ar":"مقلد","tr":"muqallid","en":"legal follower","quick":"One who follows a qualified opinion without personally performing the full juristic derivation.","worklinks":[["ihya","imitation and reason","Contextual"]]},{"id":"mubah","ar":"مباح","tr":"mubāḥ","en":"permitted","quick":"A normative option left open by the revealed address; to be distinguished from original nonliability without address.","worklinks":[["mankhul","categories of legal rulings","Topical"]]},{"id":"wajib","ar":"واجب","tr":"wājib","en":"obligatory","quick":"Demanded performance to which a legal consequence attaches upon deliberate omission.","worklinks":[["wasit","concrete obligations","Topical"]]},{"id":"mandub","ar":"مندوب","tr":"mandūb","en":"recommended","quick":"An act called for without punishment prescribed for omission.","worklinks":[["ihya","recommended devotional practice","Contextual"]]},{"id":"masad","ar":"مفسدة","tr":"mafsada","en":"corruption / injury","quick":"The frustration of a legally recognized interest or purpose; averting it can itself constitute maṣlaḥa.","worklinks":[["shifa","legal suitability and purpose","Topical"]]},{"id":"istishab","ar":"استصحاب","tr":"istiṣḥāb","en":"presumption of continuity","quick":"Reasoning that the previously established condition remains until an adequate ground of alteration is shown.","worklinks":[["mankhul","proof from original status","Topical"]]},{"id":"istislah","ar":"استصلاح","tr":"istiṣlāḥ","en":"appeal to legal interest","quick":"Inquiry into a purported maṣlaḥa, whose probative standing requires authorization from the recognized sources and purposes.","worklinks":[["shifa","judging legally suitable meanings","Topical"]]},{"id":"sabab","ar":"سبب","tr":"sabab","en":"occasion / occasioning condition","quick":"That with whose occurrence a ruling is tied, but not therefore identical with a metaphysical efficient cause or the rationale of the rule.","worklinks":[["shifa","conditions and meaningful properties","Topical"]]},{"id":"shart","ar":"شرط","tr":"sharṭ","en":"legal condition","quick":"A qualification requisite for the application or validity of a ruling, distinguished from its intrinsic cause or object.","worklinks":[["wasit","juristic conditions","Topical"]]},{"id":"sihha","ar":"صحة","tr":"ṣiḥḥa","en":"legal validity","quick":"The status of an act fulfilling the conditions for juridical effect within a particular doctrinal account.","worklinks":[["wasit","application to ritual and transactional acts","Topical"]]},{"id":"fasad","ar":"فساد","tr":"fasād","en":"invalidity / defectiveness","quick":"Failure to satisfy specified juridical requirements; some schools distinguish degrees of legal invalidity.","worklinks":[["wasit","rules governing contracts and worship","Topical"]]},{"id":"rukhsa","ar":"رخصة","tr":"rukhṣa","en":"concession","quick":"A dispensation in a legally recognized situation of hardship or exception, contrasted with the ordinary rule (ʿazīma).","worklinks":[["ihya","rites under hardship","Contextual"]]},{"id":"azima","ar":"عزيمة","tr":"ʿazīma","en":"standing rule / resolution","quick":"The ordinary normative force of a duty absent a recognized dispensing condition.","worklinks":[["wasit","ritual duties and concessions","Topical"]]},{"id":"qat_i","ar":"قطعي","tr":"qaṭʿī","en":"decisive / conclusive","quick":"A characterization of evidential decisiveness, distinct from plausible or predominant judgments.","worklinks":[["miyar","certainty of premises","Topical"]]},{"id":"maratib","ar":"مراتب","tr":"marātib","en":"ranks / degrees","quick":"Orders of priority or epistemic strength; the criteria differ between legal interests and forms of inference.","worklinks":[["shifa","suitability of reasons","Topical"]]},{"id":"adala","ar":"عدالة","tr":"ʿadāla","en":"uprightness/probity","quick":"A quality relevant to whether another person may rely upon the jurist’s fatwā; not equated by al-Ghazālī with the validity of the jurist’s own reasoning.","worklinks":[["wasit","reliability and competence in legal practice","Topical"]]},{"id":"tarjih","ar":"ترجيح","tr":"tarjīḥ","en":"reasoned preference","quick":"Evaluating competing proofs and giving precedence to the stronger indication when legally warranted; not arbitrary selection.","worklinks":[["mankhul","hierarchy of proofs and probable indicia","Topical"]]},{"id":"fatwa","ar":"فتوى","tr":"fatwā","en":"legal opinion","quick":"A juristic response offered for another person to follow or rely upon; its reception is governed by the standing of the opinion-giver.","worklinks":[["wasit","positive legal application","Topical"]]},{"id":"asl","ar":"أصل","tr":"aṣl","en":"original case / fundamental","quick":"In legal analogy, the original case from which the ruling is extended; in other contexts the term means a principle or source.","worklinks":[["asas","formal and legal analogy","Topical"]]},{"id":"far","ar":"فرع","tr":"farʿ","en":"derivative case / branch","quick":"The case newly assessed by analogy; also a subsidiary legal question in the general/particular relation.","worklinks":[["mankhul","branch and principle","Topical"]]},{"id":"taswib","ar":"تصويب","tr":"taṣwīb","en":"finding a juristic conclusion correct","quick":"The thesis about the status of a duly qualified jurist’s outcome in matters open to ijtihād; requires specifying competent agent and proper subject.","worklinks":[["mankhul","disagreement and evidence","Topical"]]},{"id":"ilm_batin","ar":"علم الباطن","tr":"ʿilm al-bāṭin","en":"inward science","quick":"Knowledge of the heart and its purification from blameworthy traits, explicitly named in al-Mustaṣfā’s classification of religious disciplines.","worklinks":[["ihya","science of practice, outward and inward","Verified primary parallel"]]}],"works":[{"id":"miyar","title":"Miʿyār al-ʿIlm","ar":"معيار العلم","domain":"Logic","url":"https://www.ghazali.org/combined/","description":"Technical treatment of concepts, inference and classes of assent."},{"id":"mihakk","title":"Miḥakk al-Naẓar","ar":"محك النظر","domain":"Logic","url":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/محك_النظر_(الغزالي)","description":"Earlier compact logical treatment that al-Ghazālī names in al-Mustaṣfā."},{"id":"qistas","title":"al-Qisṭās al-Mustaqīm","ar":"القسطاس المستقيم","domain":"Logic and scriptural argument","url":"https://www.ghazali.org/combined/","description":"Another vocabulary and presentation of valid inferential forms."},{"id":"shifa","title":"Shifāʾ al-Ghalīl","ar":"شفاء الغليل","domain":"Legal analogy and reasons","url":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/شفاء_الغليل_في_بيان_الشبه_والمخيل_ومسالك_التعليل","description":"Analysis of analogical reasoning, indicated reasons, and suitability."},{"id":"asas","title":"Asās al-Qiyās","ar":"أساس القياس","domain":"Legal analogy","url":"https://www.ghazali.org/w-jlt/","description":"Focused treatise on the foundation and forms of analogy."},{"id":"mankhul","title":"al-Mankhūl","ar":"المنخول","domain":"Early uṣūl","url":"https://www.ghazali.org/combined/","description":"Early treatment of legal theory for diachronic comparison."},{"id":"ihya","title":"Iḥyāʾ ʿUlūm al-Dīn","ar":"إحياء علوم الدين","domain":"Ethics and religious sciences","url":"https://www.ghazali.org/combined/","description":"Ethical, practical and pedagogical setting for legal formation."},{"id":"iqtisad","title":"al-Iqtiṣād fī al-Iʿtiqād","ar":"الاقتصاد في الاعتقاد","domain":"Theology","url":"https://www.ghazali.org/combined/","description":"Theological justification and modes of argument."},{"id":"mizan","title":"Mīzān al-ʿAmal","ar":"ميزان العمل","domain":"Ethics and practical reasoning","url":"https://www.ghazali.org/combined/","description":"Practical reasoning and the evaluation of action."},{"id":"munqidh","title":"al-Munqidh min al-Ḍalāl","ar":"المنقذ من الضلال","domain":"Epistemology and intellectual autobiography","url":"https://www.ghazali.org/combined/","description":"Epistemic certainty and the relation of disciplines."},{"id":"tahdhib","title":"Tahdhīb al-Uṣūl","ar":"تهذيب الأصول","domain":"Earlier legal theory","url":"https://www.ghazali.org/w-jlt/","description":"Named by al-Ghazālī in his preface as more exhaustive than al-Mustaṣfā."},{"id":"jawahir","title":"Jawāhir al-Qurʾān","ar":"جواهر القرآن","domain":"Qurʾānic hermeneutics","url":"https://www.ghazali.org/combined/","description":"Explicitly named in the author’s preface as a concise treatment of the inward path."},{"id":"kimiya","title":"Kīmiyā-yi Saʿādat","ar":"كيمياء السعادة","domain":"Practical and spiritual ethics","url":"https://www.ghazali.org/combined/","description":"Explicitly named in the author’s preface as an intermediate treatment of the inward path."},{"id":"wasit","title":"al-Wasīṭ fī al-Madhhab","ar":"الوسيط في المذهب","domain":"Shāfiʿī positive jurisprudence","url":"https://www.ghazali.org/combined/","description":"Substantive legal rulings and their doctrinal conditions. Only topical leads are included; parallel passages are not yet collated."}],"toc":[{"id":"preface","title":"Author’s preface and intellectual itinerary","ar":"خطبة الكتاب"},{"id":"prelim","title":"Opening: subject, boundaries and four axes","ar":"صدر الكتاب"},{"id":"logic","title":"Logical introduction (restored)","ar":"مقدمة الكتاب"},{"id":"hukm","title":"Axis I · Legal rulings","ar":"القطب الأول في الأحكام"},{"id":"evidence","title":"Axis II · Proofs","ar":"القطب الثاني في الأدلة"},{"id":"maslaha","title":"Axis II · Legal interests","ar":"الاستصلاح"},{"id":"inference","title":"Axis III · Interpretation and extraction","ar":"القطب الثالث في الدلالات"},{"id":"ijtihad","title":"Axis IV · Qualified inquiry","ar":"القطب الرابع في المجتهد"}],"citations":{"bulaq":"al-Ghazālī, al-Mustaṣfā, Būlāq print, 1322–1324 AH (two volumes).","hafiz":"al-Ghazālī, al-Mustaṣfā, ed. Ḥamza b. Zuhayr Ḥāfiẓ (Jeddah, four volumes).","hammad":"Ahmad Zaki Mansur Hammad, English translation of volume I, doctoral dissertation, University of Chicago, 1987; compare, do not reprint."},"scope":{"independently_translated_passages":111,"continuous_excerpts":16,"whole_work_completed":false,"all_four_axes_translated":false,"arabic_critical_apparatus_collation":"No manuscript collation","verified_historical_dictionary_entries":0,"crosswork_links_with_evidence":24,"reprint_hammad_prose":false,"source_linked_dictionary_excerpts":11,"full_historical_dictionary_articles":0,"historical_dictionary_excerpts":17,"translation_comparison_loci":8,"translated_excerpts":111},"crosswork":[{"id":"CW001","term":"usul","work":"tahdhib","relation":"direct-bibliographic","mustasfa":"P03","workQuoteAr":"","workQuoteEn":"","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/2/%D8%AE%D8%B7%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","note":"Named as longer/more exhaustive than the present work; no line-by-line terminology comparison yet."},{"id":"CW002","term":"usul","work":"mankhul","relation":"direct-bibliographic","mustasfa":"P03","workQuoteAr":"","workQuoteEn":"","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/2/%D8%AE%D8%B7%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","note":"Named as briefer than the present work; edition-to-edition conceptual comparison pending."},{"id":"CW003","term":"usul","work":"ihya","relation":"direct-bibliographic","mustasfa":"P02","workQuoteAr":"","workQuoteEn":"","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/2/%D8%AE%D8%B7%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","note":"Named directly; the relation to outward/inward disciplines needs separate passage-level study."},{"id":"CW004","term":"usul","work":"jawahir","relation":"direct-bibliographic","mustasfa":"P02","workQuoteAr":"","workQuoteEn":"","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/2/%D8%AE%D8%B7%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","note":"Named directly in the author’s preface."},{"id":"CW005","term":"usul","work":"kimiya","relation":"direct-bibliographic","mustasfa":"P02","workQuoteAr":"","workQuoteEn":"","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/2/%D8%AE%D8%B7%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","note":"Named directly in the author’s preface."},{"id":"CW006","term":"hadd","work":"miyar","relation":"direct-authorial-reference","mustasfa":"L01","workQuoteAr":"","workQuoteEn":"","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%B5%D8%B1-%D9%85%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%83-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%86%D8%B8%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86","note":"Author directs the reader to an earlier and fuller treatment of definition and demonstration."},{"id":"CW007","term":"burhan","work":"mihakk","relation":"direct-authorial-reference","mustasfa":"L01","workQuoteAr":"","workQuoteEn":"","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%B5%D8%B1-%D9%85%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%83-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D9%84%D9%88%D9%85-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%86%D8%B8%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%B1%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86","note":"Author explicitly names Miḥakk al-Naẓar for fuller logic."},{"id":"CW008","term":"qiyas","work":"asas","relation":"direct-authorial-reference","mustasfa":"L05","workQuoteAr":"","workQuoteEn":"","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamweb.org/ar/library/index.php?ID=25&bk_no=33&flag=1&page=bookcontents","note":"Author explicitly states that his earlier Asās al-Qiyās explained the qualified use of the word qiyās in the example."},{"id":"CW009","term":"maslaha","work":"shifa","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"M02","workQuoteAr":"المعاني المناسبة: ما تشير إلى وجوه المصالح وأماراتها","workQuoteEn":"Legally suitable meanings are those that point to the aspects of interests and their indications.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/شفاء_الغليل_في_بيان_الشبه_والمخيل_ومسالك_التعليل","note":"Compare with the purposes-of-law definition; this Shifāʾ passage emphasizes suitability and indications rather than identifying all legal interests with ordinary benefits."},{"id":"CW010","term":"hadd","work":"mihakk","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"L03","workQuoteAr":"فالمطلوب من المعرفة لا يقتنص إلا بالحد","workQuoteEn":"The knowledge sought in apprehending a thing is obtained only through definition.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/محك_النظر_(الغزالي)","note":"Miḥakk, introduction: knowledge of a single notion is distinguished from assent to a proposition. Compare the definitional role of ḥadd in the al-Mustaṣfā prolegomenon. Text attested, but print pagination still to be controlled."},{"id":"CW011","term":"zann","work":"mihakk","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"I01","workQuoteAr":"وهذا يسمّى ظناً وله درجات في الميل إلى الزيادة والنقصان لا تحصى","workQuoteEn":"This is called probable assent (ẓann); its degrees of increase and diminution cannot be numbered.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/محك_النظر_(الغزالي)","note":"Miḥakk, discussion of degrees of assent: ẓann is an inclination toward affirmation compatible with entertaining its opposite, strengthened by testimony and circumstantial evidence. This provides a genuine conceptual parallel, not an identity of legal and logical situations."},{"id":"CW012","term":"maqasid","work":"shifa","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"M02","workQuoteAr":"وجميع أنواع المناسبات ترجع إلى رعاية المقاصد","workQuoteEn":"Every kind of legal suitability ultimately concerns the safeguarding of purposes.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/شفاء_الغليل_في_بيان_الشبه_والمخيل_ومسالك_التعليل","note":"Shifāʾ al-Ghalīl, discussion of the suitable meaning (al-munāsib): the distinction between acquiring a sought good and preserving it against removal supplies context for the objectives-of-law account in al-Mustaṣfā."},{"id":"CW013","term":"burhan","work":"mihakk","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"L04","workQuoteAr":"","workQuoteEn":"","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/محك_النظر_(الغزالي)","note":"Miḥakk, introduction and discussion of the conditions of syllogism: al-Ghazālī expressly relates sought propositional knowledge to demonstration, distinguishing sound arrangement from the certainty of premises. The relationship is directly checked, but a quotation is deferred pending witness collation."},{"id":"CW014","term":"yaqin","work":"mihakk","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"L04","workQuoteAr":"","workQuoteEn":"","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/محك_النظر_(الغزالي)","note":"Miḥakk, discussion immediately following the degrees of assent: a syllogism from genuinely certain premises and correctly assembled produces necessary certainty. Useful for understanding the shift from demonstrative yaqīn to juristic ẓann in al-Mustaṣfā."},{"id":"CW015","term":"tasawwur","work":"mihakk","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"L09","workQuoteAr":"فإن العلوم إدراك الذوات المفردة ... وإدراك نسبة هذه المفردات بعضها إلى البعض بالنفي والإثبات","workQuoteEn":"The sciences involve grasping individual subjects and apprehending their affirmative or negative relations.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/محك_النظر_(الغزالي)","note":"Explicit near-verbatim agreement of the exposition of simple contents and predicative relation; consult the full primary text to inspect wording and local context."},{"id":"CW016","term":"hadd","work":"mihakk","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"L10","workQuoteAr":"فالمطلوب من المعرفة لا يقتنص إلا بالحد والمطلوب من العلم ... لا يقتنص إلا بالحجة والبرهان","workQuoteEn":"What must be sought in conceptual knowledge is grasped by definition, and propositional knowledge by argument and demonstration.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/محك_النظر_(الغزالي)","note":"High-confidence verbal parallel: the same methodological pairing appears in the logical manual. Al-Mustaṣfā uses the same conception/assent architecture."},{"id":"CW017","term":"burhan","work":"mihakk","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"L12","workQuoteAr":"والخلل يدخل عليه تارة من الأقاويل التي هي مقدمات القياس ... وأخرى من كيفية الترتيب والنظم","workQuoteEn":"A syllogism can go wrong because of its premises or because of its arrangement and form.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/محك_النظر_(الغزالي)","note":"Directly supports distinguishing the matter and form of reasoning; the legal-theory introduction should not be translated as if all formal inference were automatically certain."},{"id":"CW018","term":"tasdiq","work":"mankhul","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"L09","workQuoteAr":"فإن المعرفة لا تتعدى إلا إلى مفعول واحد والعلم يتعدى إلى مفعولين","workQuoteEn":"The verb of acquaintance takes one object, whereas the verb of knowing takes two.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/أبو_حامد_الغزالي_-_المنخول_في_علم_الأصول","note":"A genuine parallel in al-Mankhūl’s treatment of defining knowledge: the grammatical illustration is reused for the distinction between apprehending a term and assenting to a proposition."},{"id":"CW019","term":"usul","work":"mankhul","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"O11","workQuoteAr":"وأما الأصول فمادته الكلام والفقه واللغة","workQuoteEn":"The material of uṣūl is theology, fiqh, and language.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/أبو_حامد_الغزالي_-_المنخول_في_علم_الأصول","note":"Direct terminological parallel to the scope and sources of the discipline; compare the subsequent distinction between material studied and the final purpose of the science."},{"id":"CW020","term":"zann","work":"mankhul","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"I08","workQuoteAr":"وأخبار الآحاد ومسالك العبر والمقاييس والمستثارة بطرق الاجتهاد ليس من الأصول فإنها مظنونات بجانب أخذها مأخذ القطعيات","workQuoteEn":"Reports from individuals, paths of inference, analogies and conclusions obtained through ijtihād are not principles in the same strict sense, for they are probable when compared with things accepted as conclusive.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/أبو_حامد_الغزالي_-_المنخول_في_علم_الأصول","note":"Shows a stricter epistemic delimitation of the term uṣūl in al-Mankhūl. This should be set against the later systematic treatment of ẓann in al-Mustaṣfā, not interpreted as an absolute rejection of juristic inference."},{"id":"CW021","term":"dalala","work":"mihakk","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"L15","workQuoteAr":"القسم الأول: إن دلالة اللفظ على المعنى ينحصر في ثلاثة أوجه: وهي المطابقة والتضمن والالتزام","workQuoteEn":"Verbal indication of meaning falls under three aspects: correspondence, inclusion, and entailment.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/محك_النظر_(الغزالي)","note":"Direct logical account of indication; it provides a technical comparator to the legal modes of extracting rulings from verbal evidence."},{"id":"CW022","term":"qiyas","work":"mihakk","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"Q05","workQuoteAr":"اعلم أن القياس عبارة عن أقاويل مخصوصة أُلفت تأليفاً مخصوصاً ونُظمت نظماً مخصوصاً بشرط مخصوص يلزم منه رأي هو مطلوب الناظر","workQuoteEn":"Know that syllogism consists in specified statements compounded and ordered under definite conditions, so that the investigator’s sought conclusion follows.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/محك_النظر_(الغزالي)","note":"The qiyās of logical inference is defined as a structured argument, whereas al-Mustaṣfā I:281 defines juridical analogy through an original case, a new case and their shared ground."},{"id":"CW023","term":"kalam","work":"ihya","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"O10","workQuoteAr":"وأعني بعلم المعاملة ما يطلب منه مع الكشف العمل به والمقصود من هذا الكتاب علم المعاملة فقط دون علم المكاشفة","workQuoteEn":"By the science of practice I mean what one seeks in order to act upon it, in addition to discerning it. The concern of this book is the science of practice, not the science of unveiling.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/إحياء_علوم_الدين","note":"An authorial parallel concerning the inward discipline named in al-Mustaṣfā I:7. This is a comparison of scopes and aims, not proof that fiqh and inward purification are interchangeable."},{"id":"CW024","term":"hadd","work":"mihakk","relation":"verified-primary-parallel","mustasfa":"L16","workQuoteAr":"والمعرفة قسمان: أولى وهو الذي لا يطلب بالبحث كالمفردات المدركة بالحس، ومطلوب وهو الذي يدل اسمه منه على أمر جملي غير مفصل فيطلب تفصيله","workQuoteEn":"Conceptual acquaintance is of two kinds: primary knowledge not sought by investigation, and that sought when its name indicates a general notion whose detail remains unarticulated.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/محك_النظر_(الغزالي)","note":"Corroborates the division between primary and sought conception, with close verbal parallels in the opening of Miḥakk."}],"dictionaries":[{"term":"hadd","source":"al-Jurjānī, al-Taʿrīfāt","sourceDate":"later tradition","ar":"الحد قول دال على ماهية الشيء","en":"A definition is a statement that indicates the quiddity of a thing.","sourceUrl":"https://adab.wiki/wiki/التعريفات_(الشريف_الجرجاني)/الحد","status":"online text checked; excerpt, not full printed dictionary collation","note":"This is a later technical glossary, not al-Ghazālī’s own definition.","entryCompleteness":"excerpt"},{"term":"burhan","source":"al-Jurjānī, al-Taʿrīfāt","sourceDate":"later tradition","ar":"البرهان هو القياس المؤلف من اليقينيات سواء كانت ابتداء وهي الضروريات أو بواسطة وهي النظريات","en":"Demonstration is a syllogism composed of certainties, whether immediate—those that are necessary—or mediated—those reached through reasoning.","sourceUrl":"https://adab.wiki/wiki/التعريفات_(الشريف_الجرجاني)/البرهان","status":"online text checked; excerpt, not full printed dictionary collation","note":"Use this stricter definition to distinguish al-Ghazālī’s juridical probable premises.","entryCompleteness":"excerpt"},{"term":"zann","source":"al-Jurjānī, al-Taʿrīfāt","sourceDate":"later tradition","ar":"الظن هو الاعتقاد الراجح مع احتمال النقيض، ويستعمل في اليقين والشك","en":"Ẓann is a predominant conviction with the possibility of its opposite; the word is also used for certainty and for doubt.","sourceUrl":"https://adab.wiki/wiki/التعريفات_(الشريف_الجرجاني)/الظن","status":"online text checked; excerpt, not full printed dictionary collation","note":"A broader lexical note does not nullify al-Ghazālī’s technical degree of assent.","entryCompleteness":"excerpt"},{"term":"illah","source":"al-Jurjānī, al-Taʿrīfāt","sourceDate":"later tradition","ar":"وشرعا: عبارة عما يجب الحكم به معه","en":"In legal usage, it designates that with which a ruling must obtain.","sourceUrl":"https://adab.wiki/wiki/التعريفات_(الشريف_الجرجاني)/العلة","status":"online text checked; excerpt, not full printed dictionary collation","note":"The source distinguishes linguistic, metaphysical and juridical senses; this extracts only the legal definition.","entryCompleteness":"excerpt"},{"term":"ijma","source":"al-Jurjānī, al-Taʿrīfāt","sourceDate":"later tradition","ar":"وفي الاصطلاح: اتفاق المجتهدين في أمة محمد عليه الصلاة والسلام في عصر على أمر ديني","en":"In technical usage, the agreement of the jurists qualified for independent reasoning within the community of Muḥammad, in a given age, on a religious matter.","sourceUrl":"https://www.ibnalarabi.com/books/taarifat.php?id=1","status":"online text checked; excerpt, not full printed dictionary collation","note":"Later term definition; al-Ghazālī’s own qualification of consensus must be read separately.","entryCompleteness":"excerpt"},{"term":"ijtihad","source":"al-Jurjānī, al-Taʿrīfāt","sourceDate":"later tradition","ar":"في اللغة بذل الوسع، وفي الاصطلاح: استفراغ الفقيه الوسع ليحصل له ظن بحكم شرعي","en":"Lexically, exerting one’s capacity; technically, a jurist’s full exertion of effort so that he may arrive at a probable judgment concerning a legal ruling.","sourceUrl":"https://arabiclexicon.hawramani.com/اجتهاد/","status":"online text checked; excerpt, not full printed dictionary collation","note":"Provides a useful explicit link between effort and the epistemic status of the conclusion.","entryCompleteness":"excerpt"},{"term":"zann","source":"Ibn Manẓūr, Lisān al-ʿArab","sourceDate":"d. 711/1311","ar":"الظن شك ويقين إلا أنه ليس بيقين عيان إنما هو يقين تدبر","en":"Ẓann can designate doubt or certainty, though the latter is not the certainty of direct seeing but of reflective consideration.","sourceUrl":"https://arabiclexicon.hawramani.com/ظَنُنَ/","status":"selected source-checked lexical quotation; not full entry","entryCompleteness":"excerpt","note":"A wider range than juristic predominant probability: do not substitute this lexical range wholesale for the author’s technical argument."},{"term":"hukm","source":"Ibn Manẓūr, Lisān al-ʿArab","sourceDate":"d. 711/1311","ar":"الله سبحانه وتعالى أحكم الحاكمين، وهو الحكيم له الحكم","en":"God, exalted, is the most just of judges; He is the Wise, and judgment belongs to Him.","sourceUrl":"https://arabiclexicon.hawramani.com/حَكَم/","status":"selected source-checked lexical quotation; not full entry","entryCompleteness":"excerpt","note":"The passage concerns divine judgment in lexical and religious usage, not the precise uṣūl definition of ruling as address."},{"term":"illah","source":"Lane, Arabic–English Lexicon","sourceDate":"19th-century English lexicon","ar":"عِلَّة","en":"Cause, reason, motive; also illness, defect, excuse or pretext, according to context.","sourceUrl":"https://arabiclexicon.hawramani.com/علل/","status":"selected English lexical senses; NOT a full Arabic quotation","entryCompleteness":"short sense summary","note":"A broader Arabic lexical family. The legal operative ʿilla must be determined from the juristic passage; the “illness” sense does not define the technical cause."},{"term":"tasawwur","source":"al-Ghazālī, Miḥakk al-Naẓar (authorial parallel)","sourceDate":"earlier work of same author","ar":"إدراك الذوات المفردة","en":"The apprehension of single contents or meanings.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/محك_النظر_(الغزالي)","status":"source-linked authorial comparison, not historical dictionary entry","entryCompleteness":"short parallel","note":"Included separately from later lexica because it is significantly stronger evidence for al-Ghazālī’s own use of the concept."},{"term":"tasdiq","source":"al-Ghazālī, Miḥakk al-Naẓar (authorial parallel)","sourceDate":"earlier work of same author","ar":"إدراك نسبة هذه المفردات بعضها إلى البعض بالنفي والإثبات","en":"To apprehend an affirmative or negative relation between individual meanings.","sourceUrl":"https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/محك_النظر_(الغزالي)","status":"source-linked authorial comparison, not historical dictionary entry","entryCompleteness":"short parallel","note":"Conceptual links to the author’s logic work are not retroactively ascribed to Ibn Manẓūr or al-Jurjānī."},{"term":"zann","source":"al-Jurjānī, al-Taʿrīfāt","sourceDate":"8th/14th-century technical glossary","ar":"الظن هو الاعتقاد الراجح مع احتمال النقيض ويستعمل في اليقين والشك","en":"Ẓann is a preponderant belief that retains the possibility of its contrary; the term is also used for certainty and for doubt.","sourceUrl":"https://www.islamicbook.ws/adab/la/altarifat-001.html","status":"checked selected quotation; not complete entry","entryCompleteness":"excerpt","note":"This later definition distinguishes preponderance from both logically conclusive knowledge and equipollent doubt."},{"term":"qiyas","source":"al-Jurjānī, al-Taʿrīfāt","sourceDate":"8th/14th-century technical glossary","ar":"قول مؤلف من قضايا إذا سلمت لزم عنها لذاتها قول آخر","en":"In logic: a discourse composed of propositions which, once granted, necessitate another proposition through themselves.","sourceUrl":"https://www.ibnalarabi.com/books/taarifat.php?id=21","status":"checked selected quotation; not complete entry","entryCompleteness":"excerpt","note":"This defines the logicians’ qiyās and is not a definition of legal analogical transfer."},{"term":"qiyas","source":"al-Jurjānī, al-Taʿrīfāt","sourceDate":"8th/14th-century technical glossary","ar":"وعند أهل الأصول: القياس: إبانة مثل حكم المذكورين بمثل علته في الآخر","en":"Among scholars of legal theory: qiyās manifests a ruling like that of a named case in another case by reason of a corresponding legal ground.","sourceUrl":"https://www.ibnalarabi.com/books/taarifat.php?id=21","status":"checked selected quotation; not complete entry","entryCompleteness":"excerpt","note":"Later technical contrast to the logical sense, useful for the user’s Aristotelian comparison."},{"term":"yaqin","source":"Ibn Manẓūr, Lisān al-ʿArab","sourceDate":"d. 711/1311; lexical source","ar":"اليقين: العلم وإزاحة الشك وتحقيق الأمر","en":"Yaqīn is knowledge, the removal of doubt, and the ascertainment of a matter.","sourceUrl":"https://sunna.alifta.gov.sa/BookToc/ViewServicePage?bookId=79&mainId=786715","status":"checked lexical quotation; not complete entry","entryCompleteness":"excerpt","note":"The lexical sense is broader than the rigorous conditions of demonstrative certainty discussed by al-Ghazālī."},{"term":"zann","source":"al-Tahānawī, Kashshāf Iṣṭilāḥāt al-Funūn","sourceDate":"12th/18th-century technical lexicon","ar":"ثم إطلاق الظن على الاعتقاد الراجح هو المشهور","en":"The standard use of the term ẓann is for a belief in which one side predominates.","sourceUrl":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/9477/108","status":"checked short excerpt; not complete entry","entryCompleteness":"excerpt","note":"The entry continues through several logical and exegetical senses; only the distinguishing core clause is excerpted here."},{"term":"zann","source":"al-Maḥallī, Sharḥ al-Waraqāt (technical comparison)","sourceDate":"9th/15th-century uṣūl commentary","ar":"والظن تجويز أمرين أحدهما أظهر من الآخر عند المجوز","en":"Ẓann is taking two alternatives as possible while one of them appears more likely than the other to the person weighing them.","sourceUrl":"https://ablibrary.net/book_content/b/18684/85?lang=ar","status":"checked technical gloss, not dictionary; excerpt","entryCompleteness":"excerpt","note":"Supplementary later juristic definition; included as a comparative technical definition, not falsely classified as a historical lexical dictionary."}],"source_policy":{"arabic":"Working Arabic base: linked Islamweb online Arabic transcription. The uploaded Hammad dissertation is an English comparator, NOT an Arabic textual witness. Systematic print/manuscript collation has not been performed.","translation":"Independent new renderings of selected Arabic passages only, including larger argumentative paragraphs. Not a complete continuous translation of any of the four poles; text not present is clearly marked.","hammad":"The actual two user-uploaded Hammad dissertation scans were inspected: part 1 has 303 image pages; part 2 has 489 image pages. The first begins the analytical study and the second includes the translation of Arabic Volume I from dissertation p. 300. The comparison is selective; it does not establish exhaustive superiority.","pagination":"I:n denotes online Arabic page reference, NOT dissertation p.n. The uploaded part-2 Hammad scan begins its translation at printed dissertation p.300 = PDF page 4, hence a local PDF page lookup uses printed-page minus 296.","terms":"Quick glosses are contextual editorial explanations. Lexicon quotations are deliberately short and source-linked; this release does not imply all historic dictionaries have been imported in full.","links":"“Direct” means a work named within al-Mustaṣfā. “Verified primary parallel” means an independently inspected passage in another work; such passages are not necessarily lexical duplicates. “Topical” and “contextual” are research leads without verified textual correspondence.","crosswork":"Verbal parallels are given in Arabic and English where inspected; bibliographic mentions and topical leads are differentiated. Locators generally give source-page/online work, not fully collated edition-specific line numbers.","hammad_comparison":"Eight explicitly scoped comparison case studies align dissertation page ranges, technical-language differences, and the omitted logical introduction. They are an editorial register rather than a complete analytical collation of Hammad’s 1987 translation.","working_translation":"Every displayed English paragraph is a new independent passage rendering (some overlap in Arabic with adjacent excerpt cards). There is no complete chapter, volume or four-part continuous translation yet.","online_arabic":"Source links point to an online Arabic text of al-Mustaṣfā. The work has NOT undergone a systematic second-witness collation; even exact excerpts require further proofing against a fixed printed edition before use as a definitive edition.","note_authorship":"Explanatory and philosophical notes are editorial constructions. A cross-work quotation is shown only where a textual parallel was consulted; the rest are labelled contextual or topical research leads.","lexicon":"Some later source-linked lexical or technical definitions have been excerpted and freshly rendered into English. They are NOT full dictionary entries. Later lexicography must not be retrojected onto al-Ghazālī.","scope":"Coverage count measures displayed selected passages, not pages translated, unique paragraphs, percentage of full treatise, or definitive critical review."},"translationComparison":[{"printedPage":"304–305","target":"P05, P06, P01","topic":"Reason and transmitted authority","hammadTreatment":"The supplied scan uses “Reason and Tradition” at this point.","newEdition":"Distinguishes ʿaql from samʿ, and raʾy from sharʿ, with context-sensitive notes.","assessment":"terminological reconsideration; not a claim of mistranslation without qualification"},{"printedPage":"307–310","target":"O08, O09","topic":"Definition of uṣūl, subject versus proof","hammadTreatment":"The translation presents fiqh and uṣūl as the study of rulings and their sources.","newEdition":"Separates a ruling, its proof, and modes of its general indication; makes the general/particular distinction explicit.","assessment":"additional argument mapping rather than simply stylistic replacement"},{"printedPage":"315–316","target":"F05, F06","topic":"Four axes and legal fruit","hammadTreatment":"Uses the four poles and the metaphor of fruits and their extraction.","newEdition":"Keeps the metaphor while consistently cross-linking fruit, proof, inference and juristic agent.","assessment":"terminological and navigational improvement"},{"printedPage":"321–323","target":"L06, L07, L09, L10","topic":"Missing introduction to logic","hammadTreatment":"The dissertation’s translator note 15 on p.323 says the separate logical introduction is omitted.","newEdition":"Restores selected primary Arabic–English passages and links them to Miḥakk al-Naẓar, Miʿyār al-ʿIlm and al-Mankhūl.","assessment":"documented substantive recovery; not the complete logical prolegomenon"},{"printedPage":"325–331","target":"H01, H02, H03","topic":"Good and bad before revelation","hammadTreatment":"Prints the opening discussion of ḥusn and qubḥ and the debate with the Muʿtazila.","newEdition":"Separates ordinary utility, ethical appraisal, and legally authoritative status and warns against conflating them.","assessment":"critical explanatory improvement"},{"printedPage":"351–356","target":"H04, H05","topic":"The five categories of rulings","hammadTreatment":"Distinguishes wajib, mamnūʿ/maḥẓūr, mubāḥ, mandūb and makrūh.","newEdition":"Renders the five consistently and places the norm’s force beside the sentence establishing it.","assessment":"terminology control across passages"},{"printedPage":"430–434","target":"Q02, Q03","topic":"Sabab, ʿilla and legal reason","hammadTreatment":"Renders the relevant discussion of sabab as “reason” in the translated text.","newEdition":"Separates condition, operative legal reason, occasion and purpose; prevents any of these from automatically becoming an efficient cause.","assessment":"philosophical and juristic precision improvement"},{"printedPage":"443–446","target":"H06, M03","topic":"ʿAzīma, rukhṣa and hardship","hammadTreatment":"Uses “resolution” and “concession” in the translated material.","newEdition":"Treats these as relational legal statuses and distinguishes a dispensation from unrestricted optional choice.","assessment":"editorial semantic guidance; section-specific fresh translation remains to be completed"}],"arabicStructuralIndex":[{"part":"preface","label":"Authorial preface (خطبة الكتاب)","url":"https://gcp.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/2/%D8%AE%D8%B7%D8%A8%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","state":"selected translated extracts"},{"part":"prelim","label":"Introductory orientation and four axes (صدر الكتاب)","url":"https://gcp.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/3/%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%AD%D8%AF-%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%81%D9%82%D9%87","state":"selected translated extracts"},{"part":"logic","label":"Logic: definition and demonstration (مقدمة الكتاب)","url":"https://gcp.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/8/%D9%85%D9%82%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%A8","state":"selected translated extracts; longer chapters unrendered"},{"part":"hukm","label":"Rulings and their divisions (القطب الأول)","url":"https://gcp.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/37/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%82%D8%B7%D8%A8-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%88%D9%84-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%A9-%D9%88%D9%87%D9%8A-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AD%D9%83%D9%85-%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%83%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%85-%D9%81%D9%8A%D9%87-%D9%8A%D9%86%D9%82%D8%B3%D9%85-%D8%A5%D9%84%D9%89-%D9%81%D9%86%D9%88%D9%86-%D8%A3%D8%B1%D8%A8%D8%B9%D8%A9","state":"selected translated extracts"},{"part":"evidence","label":"The Book, Sunna, consensus and disputed sources (القطب الثاني)","url":"https://gcp.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/127/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AF%D9%84%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A5%D8%AC%D9%85%D8%A7%D8%B9-%D9%88%D9%81%D9%8A%D9%87-%D8%A3%D8%A8%D9%88%D8%A7%D8%A8","state":"selected translated extracts"},{"part":"maslaha","label":"Legal interests (الاستصلاح)","url":"https://gcp.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/159/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D8%B9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%B5%D9%88%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D9%88%D9%87%D9%88%D9%85%D8%A9-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%B5%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AD","state":"selected translated extracts"},{"part":"inference","label":"Indication and analogy (القطب الثالث)","url":"https://gcp.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/280/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B7%D8%B1%D9%81-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AB%D8%A7%D9%86%D9%8A-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A7%D9%86-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AA%D8%AF%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%AC-%D9%81%D9%8A-%D9%85%D9%86%D8%A7%D8%B2%D9%84-%D9%87%D8%B0%D9%87-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D9%82%D9%8A%D8%B3%D8%A9-%D9%85%D9%86-%D8%A3%D8%B9%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%87%D8%A7-%D8%A5%D9%84%D9%89-%D8%A3%D8%AF%D9%86%D8%A7%D9%87%D8%A7","state":"selected translated extracts"},{"part":"ijtihad","label":"Qualified reasoning and taqlīd (القطب الرابع)","url":"https://gcp.islamweb.net/ar/library/content/33/301/","state":"selected translated extracts"}],"workContexts":[{"theme":"Definition, demonstration, and legal probability","mustasfa":["L14","L15","L16","L17","I08"],"works":["mihakk","miyar","mankhul"],"editorial":"What makes demonstration certain, and what kind of assent is sufficient for legal practice?"},{"theme":"Legal analogy and logical syllogism","mustasfa":["Q05","Q06","Q07"],"works":["mihakk","asas","shifa","mankhul"],"editorial":"Distinguish formal entailment, common legal ground, suitability, and discretionary inference."},{"theme":"Moral formation and the bounds of jurisprudence","mustasfa":["O10","O11","M06","M07"],"works":["ihya","mizan","jawahir"],"editorial":"How does the science of legal evidence relate to the sciences of the heart and of practice?"},{"theme":"Competence, integrity, and institutional authority","mustasfa":["I07","I08","I09","I12"],"works":["mankhul","wasit"],"editorial":"What distinguishes a valid personal inference from the publicly reliable fatwā and a stable judgment?"}],"wholeArabic":{"witness":"OpenITI 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","source":"https://github.com/OpenITI/0525AH/blob/master/data/0505Ghazali/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa/0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1","json":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/gnamma-research/booksite/master/arabic/full-arabic-pages.json","license":"CC BY-NC-SA 4.0","pages":381,"paragraphs":2977,"translation":"not complete","note":"The full Arabic reading witness must not be confused with translated English coverage.","publishedPath":"../../../arabic/full-arabic-pages.json","licenseAttribution":"OpenITI/0525AH, 0505Ghazali.Mustasfa.Shamela0005459-ara1, normalized into page/paragraph JSON for navigation."},"scholarship":[{"id":"opwis2019","title":"Syllogistic Logic in Islamic Legal Theory: al-Ghazālī’s Arguments for the Certainty of Legal Analogy (Qiyās)","author":"Felicitas Opwis","work":"Philosophy and Jurisprudence in the Islamic World, ed. Peter Adamson (Berlin: De Gruyter, 2019)","pages":"93–112","url":"https://doi.org/10.1515/9783110552386-007","status":"user-supplied full academic source studied"},{"id":"rudolph2019","title":"Al-Ghazālī on Philosophy and Jurisprudence","author":"Ulrich Rudolph","work":"Philosophy and Jurisprudence in the Islamic World, ed. Peter Adamson (Berlin: De Gruyter, 2019)","pages":"67–91","url":"https://doi.org/10.1515/9783110552386","status":"user-supplied full academic source used"},{"id":"hallaq1997","title":"A History of Islamic Legal Theories: An Introduction to Sunnī Uṣūl al-Fiqh","author":"Wael B. Hallaq","work":"Cambridge University Press, 1997","pages":"see chapters on epistemology and logic","url":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/history-of-islamic-legal-theories/","status":"user-supplied full book; commentary reference"},{"id":"hammad1987","title":"Abū Ḥāmid al-Ghazālī’s Juristic Doctrine in al-Mustaṣfā min ʿIlm al-Uṣūl","author":"Ahmad Zaki Mansur Hammad","work":"University of Chicago dissertation, March 1987","pages":"study and the translation from printed p.300","url":"","status":"user-uploaded page scans; not independently collated throughout"}],"termScholarship":{"qiyas":[{"term":"qiyās","decision":"Contextually “juridical analogical inference” or “syllogistic inference,” with qiyās retained where its form is contested. Avoid constant unqualified “analogy.”","reason":"The formal structure of qiyās and the certainty of premises are separate questions. A formally conclusive schema can yield only probable juristic judgment when a premise is probable.","sources":["Opwis 2019, pp. 93–112","Hallaq, A History of Islamic Legal Theories, discussions of logic"]}],"illah":[{"term":"ʿilla","decision":"Usually “operative legal ground” or “effective legal attribute” rather than unqualified “cause.”","reason":"An ʿilla’s role in transferring a rule must be justified by textual indication or accepted legal inference. It is not automatically an efficient cause in natural philosophy, nor the ultimate wisdom of the rule.","sources":["Opwis 2019, discussion of legal and syllogistic qiyās"]}],"zann":[{"term":"ẓann","decision":"“Predominant judgment,” “preponderant probability,” or “probable assent,” depending on the argument.","reason":"Technical ẓann can license a practical conclusion without converting the evidence into certainty. “Opinion” alone risks concealing that norm-governed epistemic status.","sources":["Opwis 2019, pp. 103–108","Hallaq 1997, epistemology of legal reasoning"]}],"burhan":[{"term":"burhān","decision":"“Demonstration” if logical matter/form is in view; “conclusive proof” if describing the evidence standard.","reason":"The premises’ certitude and syllogistic validity must each be considered; juridically accepted qiyās is not automatically burhān in a strict apodictic sense.","sources":["Opwis 2019, pp. 93–112","Rudolph 2019, pp. 67–91"]}],"dalala":[{"term":"dalāla","decision":"“Indication” or “mode of signification” rather than a generic “meaning.”","reason":"Modes of indication are a formal object of legal theory and distinguish wording, implicature, required presupposition, and an inferable intelligible meaning.","sources":["al-Mustaṣfā, Opening, four axes","Hallaq 1997"]}],"hukm":[{"term":"ḥukm","decision":"“Legal ruling” for a normative judgment; “legal qualification” for a characteristic predicated of acts; retain ḥukm at disputed category borders.","reason":"The divine khiṭāb is distinguished from the physical act and its contingent properties.","sources":["al-Mustaṣfā, Opening and Axis I","Hammad 1987, translation beginning p. 300"]}],"khitab":[{"term":"khiṭāb","decision":"“Address” or “divine normative address,” not merely “speech.”","reason":"Its juridical function connects what is commanded or forbidden with those capable of understanding the address.","sources":["al-Mustaṣfā, Axis I","Hammad 1987"]}],"fiqh":[{"term":"fiqh","decision":"“Jurisprudence” for the developed disciplinary sense; “understanding” for the original lexical sense.","reason":"The author contrasts juristic knowledge of legally qualified acts with theological predicates of the same acts.","sources":["al-Mustaṣfā, Opening definition","Hallaq 1997"]}],"usul":[{"term":"uṣūl al-fiqh","decision":"“Principles of jurisprudence” in the title and disciplinary definitions; “legal theory” as an explanatory register where needed.","reason":"It treats the general authorization of evidence and its modes of indication, not a catalogue of particular rulings.","sources":["al-Mustaṣfā, Opening definition","Hallaq 1997"]}],"maslaha":[{"term":"maṣlaḥa","decision":"“Recognized legal interest” or “welfare” contextually, rather than a bare “utility.”","reason":"The technical argument invokes purposes of revealed law and ranks of interest, not simply whatever agents find advantageous.","sources":["al-Mustaṣfā, discussion of maṣlaḥa","Hallaq 1997"]}]}};